const RULES={"D3.22":["Reinforcement and repair","Shield boxes lost in earlier turns can be repaired by Damage Control (two energy per box), continuous damage control (a rule not used here) or repair systems. Emergency damage repair cannot repair shields. (Reinforcement itself is D3.34.)",["D3.34","D9.21"]],"S5.1":["Local conditions chart","Optional (S5.0): at the start of a scenario roll two dice. 2 black hole, 3 nebula, 4 planet and moon, 5 wandering monster, 6 asteroid belt, 7 empty space, 8 old minefield, 9 pirate, 10 gas giant, 11 variable pulsar, 12 roll again twice. This game builds 4 (planet, no moon), 6, 7 and 10; the others are rolled again.",["S5.2"]],"S5.2":["Explanation of results","4: class-M planet in 2420 (moon in 1514). 10: gas giant in 1815, radius by one die (1 or 2 count as 3), a one-hex gap, then rings two hexes wide. 12: two more results; a second 12 is ignored; 4 with 10 drops 4; 6 with 4 or 10 drops 6. A scenario that sets its own terrain does not use the chart (S5.31).",["S5.1"]],"P2.211":["Class-M planet","A class-M planet fills one hex. No weapons may be fired through it (P2.211) and units cannot enter it except to land (P2.212). This game stops a ship at the planet hex instead of crash-landing it.",["P2.212"]],"P2.212":["Entering a planet hex","Units cannot enter a planet hex unless they are landing on it or in low flight. Entering any other way is a crash landing. This game does not model landing or crashing: the ship just holds its position, and the computer steers clear.",[]],"P2.224":["Gas giants","Units cannot enter any hex of a gas giant or its atmosphere (a catastrophic landing in the rules). Here the ship is stopped at the edge. The giant blocks fire like a planet.",["P2.223"]],"P2.223":["Rings","Rings are asteroid-like hexes but use the ring damage table: speed 1-6 none; 7-14 1/3/5 on a 4/5/6; 15-25 1/3/5/7 on 3-6; 26+ 2/5/7/10/15 on 2-6. The ring ECM of half a point per hex is not modelled.",["P3.2"]],"SG4.0":["Basic Piracy","An Orion pirate raids a convoy of freighters (3 to 6), optionally guarded by an escort of the smallest warship or police ship of the convoy's race (SG4.47), with a relief cruiser that may arrive from turn 6 (SG4.46). The pirate wins only by capturing a freighter (SG4.5). In this game you fly the pirate and the computer flies the convoy. Captain's Basic Set page 210.",["S2.2"]],
"SG6.0":["Pursuit into the Asteroids","A Navy cruiser chases a pirate towards its home base through an asteroid field laid out by P3.1 (SG6.2): the pirate base in 4017, the CR in 0517 heading B at maximum speed, and the Navy cruiser in 0327 heading B at maximum speed. The book starts the CR and the cruiser at Weapon Status III; this game starts everyone at Weapon Status II. Any edge may be used to disengage. Scored by the SG6.5 points. Captain's Basic Set page 211.",["S2.2"]],
"D1.5":["Friendly fire","Units may not fire on a friendly unit or guide weapons at one, except in the cases of D1.51 to D1.53. Players are never required to use friendly fire. Computer ships never use it; allies and enemies only pick targets on the other side.",["D1.51","D1.52","D1.53"]],
"D1.51":["Self-destruction","A friendly ship may be fired on if it is eligible for self-destruction under D5.51: all but two of its crew units are gone, the enemy have more boarding parties aboard than it has, or no other friendly ship is in play. Crew units are counted here as general crew plus half the boarding parties.",["D1.5","D5.51"]],
"D1.52":["Capture","A friendly unit may be fired on to prevent its capture: D1.521 (being boarded), D1.522 (your other units are disengaging and it cannot) or D1.523 (held in an enemy tractor beam for a whole turn).",["D1.521","D1.522","D1.523","D1.524"]],
"D1.521":["Danger of capture by boarding","Needs an enemy ship within transporter range with boarding parties, a destroyed shield facing that ship, and enemy boarding parties aboard equal to 75% of the unit's own (150% waives the first two). The computer never boards, so this only arises from boarding the game itself allows.",["D1.52"]],
"D1.522":["Disengaging and cannot","Other friendly units are disengaging and the unit cannot. The disengaging player announces it (button in the target row) and may then fire only at that unit until it is destroyed, then must disengage at once. The game treats an ally as unable to disengage if its warp power is below the disengagement need or an enemy tractor holds it.",["D1.52"]],
"D1.523":["Held in a tractor beam","An enemy tractor beam has held the unit for at least one complete turn (32 impulses here) and it has not broken free.",["D1.52"]],
"D1.524":["Captured ships","A ship captured by enemy boarding parties becomes an enemy ship and can be fired on freely. The computer never boards.",["D1.52"]],
"D1.53":["Friendly seeking weapons","Friendly drones and plasma torpedoes (yours or an ally's) can be fired on without restriction; they are listed in the fire table marked friendly.",["D1.5"]],
"G7.36":["Tractoring a moving ship","When two ships linked by a tractor beam are both generating movement points, each ship has a pseudo-speed: its warp energy divided by the total movement cost of both ships (the smaller ship's impulse power is ignored). Each ship moves both ships whenever its pseudo-speed calls for movement, and turns by its Turn Mode at that pseudo-speed.",[]],"G7.36-C":["Both ships move on one impulse","If both ships' movements would return each to its starting hex, neither moves. If each would move two hexes, the larger ship's movement is done first and the other ship's movement is done on the next impulse, as originally stated. Movement points owed are carried from impulse to impulse (and across turns) in the order earned, and are lost if the link is released.",[]],"C2.46":["Pseudo speed","Units towing other units use their pseudo speed to determine when each of them will move the entire combination. Fractional points of movement are lost.",[]],"G10.111":["Linear web between two ships","A linear web runs between two anchor points, which may be asteroids, anchor buoys or ships. If a web is anchored in an asteroid hex, simply laying a web in that hex anchors it.",[]],"G10.112":["Single-ship web","If one ship lays the web it must begin in a hex with a valid web anchor, such as an asteroid, anchoring one end of the web to it.",[]],"G10.113":["Web dissolves","If at any time a segment of web is not anchored at both ends, it dissolves instantly.",[]],"G10.1162":["Laying ship as anchor","A ship laying web is the anchor of that web until it passes the duty on. If it moves out of a web hex without laying web in the new hex, it gives up anchor status.",[]],"G10.1314":["Terrain as anchors","Large and small asteroids, moons and planets without atmosphere can serve as web anchors.",[]],"G10.211":["Laying web","To lay web in a hex, the laying ship moves into it and spends six units of energy from any source. Newly laid web has a strength of zero. Every hex must be adjacent to another hex of the same web.",[]],"G10.212":["Reinforcing web","A Tholian ship with an operable web generator that is in a hex of the web or adjacent to it may add energy to the web.",[]],"G10.2121":["Reinforcement limit","No more than four units of energy may be added to a web during each impulse by a single ship. Any number of qualified ships can reinforce on the same impulse.",[]],"G10.22":["Web restrictions","Webs may not cross over or connect, or they are considered a single web. No web hex may be adjacent to more than two other hexes of its own web.",[]],"G10.232":["Web length","No web can be more than 30 hexes long.",[]],"G10.3":["Strength of webs","The strength is a function of the energy used to reinforce it and its size. Initial laying gives no strength. The maximum strength of any web hex is 35.",[]],"G10.31":["Strength procedure","Total the reinforcing energy, deduct deterioration, and divide by the number of hexes, ignoring fractions. The ignored fractions are not discarded; they accumulate in the total.",[]],"G10.41":["Deterioration","At the end of each turn a web loses one point of aggregate energy for each hex of web. (The full rule makes this 0.667 per hex in Y161-174 and 0.50 from Y175 because of G10.32; this game uses the flat one point.)",[]],"G10.43":["New web","A newly laid web hex is at zero strength. It dissolves in seven turns (224 impulses) if not reinforced to strength one. This also applies to web that has deteriorated to zero.",[]],"G10.51":["Ships in webs","A ship that enters a web hex ceases movement until it leaves the hex by the rules below or the web deteriorates to zero strength.",[]],"G10.511":["Leaving a web","A ship other than a Tholian must spend, over a period of 32 consecutive impulses, movement points equal to the strength of the web. It then moves out in the direction of movement on the last such impulse; it can turn but cannot leave by a sideslip.",[]],"G10.52":["Small units","Shuttles, fighters and seeking weapons move through a web with the same procedure as ships. If the strength exceeds their speed they stay trapped. A seeking weapon entering a hex that holds its target strikes at once.",[]],"G10.53":["Tholian units","Tholian units move through webs without spending extra energy.",[]],"G10.55":["Launch","Anything launched from a ship in a web hex is caught by the web until it spends enough movement to escape, as if it had entered from a non-web hex.",[]],"G10.57":["Maneuvers while caught","A ship caught in a web cannot use erratic maneuvers. It can use emergency deceleration, high energy turns and tactical maneuvers, and it can turn by the turn mode for its speed even though it is not moving.",[]],"G10.59":["Deceleration by webs","When a ship enters a web it in effect decelerates, and a violent deceleration can damage it.",[]],"G10.591":["Breakdown on impact","If the ship loses twelve or more movement points (speed twelve or more and web strength twelve or more) it rolls for breakdown as if it had made a high energy turn.",[]],"G10.592":["Impact damage","Each point of speed lost beyond the first twelve causes one point of damage on the shield that entered the web first, never more than the movement points the web countered.",[]],"G10.593":["Drones and shuttles","Drones and shuttles take one point of damage for each point of speed lost over twenty. Seeking weapons strike their target before this is applied.",[]],"G10.61":["Non-Tholian weapons","No direct-fire weapon may be fired through a web hex. Direct fire may go into or out of a web hex. If the web has zero strength this rule does not apply.",[]],"G10.62":["Tholian weapons","Tholian ships may fire phasers (not other weapons) through their own web hexes. Each phaser scores one point less for each hex between the firing ship and the farthest web hex the line of fire passes through.",[]],"G10.71":["Transporters and tractors","Transporters and tractor beams cannot function through web hexes. They may work into or out of web hexes, or between adjacent web hexes.",[]],"S1.43":["Fixed and floating maps","In the case of a floating map, if units are about to move off the edge of the map, simply move every unit ten hexes away from the edge and allow the battle to continue.",[]],"FP7.1":["Plasma shotgun designation","Any torpedo larger than a type-F can fire a number of type-F torpedoes simultaneously: R 5, S 3, G 2. The additional arming energy builds magnetic fields in the arming chamber to subdivide the torpedo.",[]],"FP7.21":["Simultaneous launch","All of the type-Fs must be fired on the same impulse and with the same facing.",[]],"FP7.22":["Separate targets","Each type-F must have a separate target, and those targets must all be within the tracking arc of the launcher. If there are not enough targets, the excess are removed from play.",[]],"FP7.23":["Two launchers","A ship cannot fire two plasma launchers in the same impulse if one of them is a shotgun.",[]],"FP7.31":["Shotgun decision","The decision to fire a plasma charge as a shotgun is made during Energy Allocation of the last turn of arming. It cannot be both an enveloping and a shotgun type.",[]],"FP7.32":["Shotgun energy","The energy cost for the last turn of arming is double the normal cost (type-G: 2-2-6).",[]],"FP7.33":["Held torpedo","A completed plasma torpedo held from a previous turn cannot be converted to a shotgun load.",[]],"FP7.41":["No holding","A launcher loaded as a shotgun cannot be held.",[]],"FP7.44":["Type-F only","Division into type-F plasma torpedoes is the only means of dividing a plasma torpedo.",[]],"D7.8":["Hit and run raids","Boarding parties may be used on hit-and-run raids: the party attempts to destroy some key item of equipment on the enemy ship. A die is rolled on the chart for the box the party attacks.",[]],"D7.81":["Raid procedure","For each boarding party making an attempt, designate the specific SSD box attacked and roll a die. Normal crew: 1 system destroyed and the party returns, 2 both destroyed, 3 to 5 party destroyed and the system is fine, 6 party returns and the system is fine.",[]],"D7.821":["One raid per party","Any boarding party may make one raid per turn, assuming transporters are available. Each transporter supports one raid, performing both the in and the out operation.",[]],"D7.823":["Raid targets","Boarding parties can attack sensor and scanner boxes but not damage control or excess damage boxes, and cannot cause critical hits.",[]],"D7.84":["Multiple raids","Two or more raids cannot be made by the same player on a single box within one eighth of a turn.",[]],"G7.6":["Extended range","Tractor beams may be operated at up to three hexes range, but this requires additional power: twice as much at range two, three times as much at range three. One point of negative tractor power cancels three points of tractor energy from a ship three hexes away.",[]],"G7.7":["Rotation","A ship holding another object in a tractor beam, if the tractor is maintained from the previous turn, can conduct a rotation during the Initial Activity Phase. Rotation changes the position of a unit, not its facing.",[]],"G7.71":["Rotation procedure","A held unit can be rotated one hex per turn, at the start of the turn after the tractor is re-established. It must remain within three hexes of the tractoring ship.",[]],"G7.73":["Rotation: facing","Rotation moves the held unit but does not change its facing.",[]],"G7.74":["Pulling","An object can be pulled into the same hex as the tractoring ship by rotation, or if tractored at extended range, pulled closer.",[]],"C12.37":["Reversing direction","A ship can switch from forward to reverse movement without paying a braking cost by plotting a speed change to speed zero, followed eight impulses later (six for a nimble ship) by a speed change to some other speed in the opposite direction.",[]],"FP1.13":["Downloading plasma","A plasma launcher may load a less powerful torpedo than it is rated for (down to type-F), but never a larger one. Downloading is chosen as the torpedo is arming and cannot be changed once it is armed.",[]],"FP1.131":["Downloading while arming","A launcher that has paid 2 + 2 can finish as its own type or as a smaller non-F type; an F needs one point on each of the first two turns.",[]],"FP1.93":["Accelerated arming","On the second turn of arming a G, S or R launcher (2 + 2 paid), two points of reserve power make a type-F that must be launched immediately.",[]],"FP1.96":["Raising a torpedo with reserve power","On the last turn of arming, reserve power can raise the torpedo up to the launcher's own type; it can still be held. A held torpedo can also be raised, but then it cannot be held past that turn (FP1.961). A type-F cannot be raised (FP1.962).",[]],"FP1.961":["Raising a held torpedo","The raised torpedo cannot be held past the end of that turn.",[]],"FP1.963":["Committing a type-F to a larger type","Reserve power added to the point that begins a type-F in a larger launcher commits it to a three-turn G, S or R.",[]],"FP1.964":["No extra holding cost on raising","Raising a held torpedo with reserve power does not require the larger torpedo's holding cost that turn.",[]],"FP2.41":["Plasma-F","A plasma-F costs nothing to hold in a type-F launcher, and one point when it was armed in a larger launcher.",[]],"C5.51":["Tactical maneuvers after emergency deceleration","After coming to a halt by emergency deceleration a ship can make warp tactical maneuvers, paid only with reserve warp or reserve impulse power. Maneuvers allocated in advance are treated as unspent movement energy (C8.1). The first cannot be made until four impulses after the halt (C5.532).",[]],"C5.52":["Temporary halts","If at speed zero during part of a turn, the ship can make tactical maneuvers during that period, paid for in energy allocation or with reserve power.",[]],"C5.532":["Tactical maneuvers after a halt","The ship earns its tactical maneuvers on the Speed 4 column but cannot make the first one until four impulses after coming to a halt.",[]],"C12.24":["Non-plotted acceleration","With reserve warp power a ship can speed up without having plotted it. The power must generate movement points equal to double the hexes gained, but not more than if the new higher speed had been kept for the rest of the turn, and not less than one hex of movement energy.",[]],"C12.244":["Partial-turn acceleration","An unplotted acceleration can last for only part of the remaining turn (at least eight impulses, six for nimble ships); the ship then drops back to its plotted speed.",[]],"G15.23":["Impulse engines","Orion ships can also double the output of their impulse engines. For size-3 ships, one impulse engine box is marked off if any or all of the impulse engine boxes run at double output.",[]],"G15.231":["Impulse doubling, size 3","One impulse engine box is marked off if any or all of the impulse boxes run doubled.",[]],"E2.251":["Low-power phasers","Any Ph-1, Ph-2 or Ph-4 can be fired as a Ph-3 by using only 1/2 unit of power.",[]],"SG6.5":["Victory conditions", "The Orion scores 1 point if the cruiser disengages, 2 if crippled, 5 if destroyed, 10 if captured. The Navy scores 1 if the CR is crippled, 3 if destroyed, 10 if captured, 4 if the base is crippled, 20 if destroyed or captured. The higher score wins.", []],"SG6.41":["Map", "The real map is fixed and any unit can leave it in any direction; a unit that leaves has disengaged and cannot return.", []],"SG6.2":["Initial set-up", "Terrain: an asteroid field by P3.1. Pirate: base station in 4017 (WS-I), Orion CR in 0517 heading B at maximum speed (WS-III). Navy: a cruiser in 0327 heading B at maximum speed (WS-III).", []],"R1.3":["Base station (BS)", "The Base Station SSD: shields of 30 boxes per facing, six Ph-4 and three Ph-3, no warp engines, power from 22 reactor boxes, explosion strength 11, BPV 120. The box layout here is synthetic; counts were read from the SSD.", []],"E2.14":["Type IV heavy phaser", "A very powerful phaser used only on bases (with stabilizers). It costs two units of power to fire one time and reaches range 100.", []],"C3.72":["Performing rotations", "Rate 2 rotates on impulses 12 and 28.", []],"C3.71":["Setting rotation rate", "The owner sets the rate before the scenario: not less than one 60-degree turn each turn, not more than one each eight impulses.", []],"C3.7":["Base rotation", "A base cannot move but can rotate: at most one 60-degree turn every eight impulses and at least one a turn. The computer base turns right at impulses 12 and 28.", []],"P3.33":["Electronic warfare", "Each asteroid hex between the firing unit and the target, including both end hexes, gives the target one point of natural ECM, which ECCM can counter.", []],"P3.221":["Nimble units", "Nimble units subtract one from the asteroid damage die roll (minimum 1).", []],"P3.21":["Direction of damage", "The damage goes on shield #1 when moving forward, shield #4 in reverse; sideslips make no difference.", []],"P3.2":["Effect of asteroids on movement", "Every asteroid hex a ship enters needs a die roll for collision damage, by speed: 1-6 none; 7-14 2, 6 or 10 on a roll of 4, 5 or 6; 15-25 3, 6, 10, 15 on 3 to 6; 26+ 5, 10, 15, 20, 30 on 2 to 6. Leaving an asteroid hex costs nothing (P3.26).", []],"P3.12":["Asteroid hexes", "All hexes within two hexes of an asteroid counter are asteroid hexes. Overlapping counters do not make 'double' asteroids.", []],"P3.11":["Layout of an asteroid field", "One asteroid counter is placed in each of 0505, 0713, 1007, 0522, 0730, 1024, 1905, 2113, 2407, 1922, 2128, 2424, 3322, 3513, 3807, 3305, 3528 and 3824. Each counter then moves one hex in a random direction (a die roll).", []],"SG4.41":["Map", "The real scenario uses a floating map and any unit can disengage in any direction. This game uses its fixed map.", []],"SG4.5":["Victory conditions", "The convoy player receives 10 points for each large freighter and 5 for each small freighter in his convoy, less the escort penalty, plus the Modified Victory Conditions points. If the Orion is captured the convoy wins automatically. The Orion player loses if he does not capture at least one freighter.", []],"SG4.47":["Escort", "The convoy player may have one escort: the smallest warship available from his race. He pays a penalty of one third of its BPV.", []],"SG4.46":["Reinforcements", "From turn 6 the convoy rolls a die each turn before Energy Allocation. On a 1 the SOS has been received and a relief force arrives: one cruiser (or two frigates or destroyers) of the convoy's race, friendly to the convoy. Here it is one cruiser, placed in the map corner farthest from the convoy.", []],"SG4.45":["Convoy cohesion", "Every ship of the convoy must stay within 10 hexes of every other ship of the convoy (ships captured by the Orion do not count). The computer freighters keep together by heading the same way but the game does not enforce the limit.", []],"SG4.3":["Length of scenario", "The scenario lasts until all forces of one side have been destroyed, captured or have disengaged.", []],"SG4.2":["Initial set-up", "Three to six freighters start in hexes 2316, 2117, 1918, 2519, 2320 and 2121, all heading B at speed 6. One escort, if wanted, starts within three hexes of a freighter. The pirate Orion enters from any map edge at a heading and speed of its choice.", []],"G15.11":["Avoid capture", "An Orion ship never surrenders; when it cannot disengage it self-destructs rather than accept capture. With the D5 option on, the computer Orion does this the turn after it would have conceded.", []],"G15.12":["Suicide bomb", "Orion ships carry a nuclear suicide bomb, already included in the explosion strength of the ship.", []],"D5.12":["Explosion", "A ship destroyed in combat explodes immediately, using the self-destruction procedure to find the extent of the blast.", []],"D5.1":["Declaring self-destruction", "A player marks the Energy Allocation Form 'self-destruct' at the start of the turn, after the other players have finished theirs. The ship does nothing else and explodes in the Self-Destruction Phase (#3) of the turn, before movement. Option D5, Advanced.", []],"D5.2":["Self-destruction force", "A ship that self-destructs, or takes an excess damage hit after all excess damage boxes are gone, explodes with the strength printed for it (the EX box on the SSD here). It is not reduced for unused weapons or dropped warp engines. An Orion's value includes its nuclear suicide bomb.", []],"D5.31":["Ship in same hex", "Another ship in the exploding ship's hex that is destroyed by the blast adds its own basic explosion strength to the original, as one combined explosion. Its shield facing is rolled (D3.42).", []],"D5.41":["Basic explosion strength (BES)", "BES 9 or less affects only the hex of the exploding unit; 10 or more also the six hexes around it. Every unit in the zone takes the BES on the shield facing the exploding unit.", []],"D5.51":["Crew units", "In multi-ship scenarios a ship may not self-destruct unless all but two of its crew units are dead. Not used here: you fly one ship.", []],"D4.43":["Explosions", "Ships explode at the instant of destruction.", []],"G15.1":["No surrender", "An Orion ship never surrenders. When it cannot disengage it self-destructs rather than accept capture (G15.11); its nuclear suicide bomb is part of its explosion strength (G15.12). Without the D5 option the crippled raider simply concedes.", []],"G15.2":["Doubling engine output", "Orion ships can double the output of each warp engine separately. The doubled power must be allocated in the Energy Allocation Phase.", []],"G15.202":["Engine box lost", "Each doubled engine loses one box at the end of the turn, in addition to any combat damage.", []],"G15.212":["Large Orion ships", "A size-3 ship such as the CR loses one box for each engine it doubles, so it can lose two warp boxes a turn.", []],"G15.26":["Speed limit", "An Orion cannot generate more than 30 movement points with warp power, even with doubled engines.", []],"G15.8":["Stealth bonus", "Most Orion warships have a built-in ECM bonus (+2 on the CR). It counts as ECM against direct fire and seeking weapons.", []],"G15.82":["Losing the bonus", "The stealth bonus is lost for as long as any warp engine is doubled.", []],"G15.4":["Optional weapon mounts", "This game fits the Raider with three disruptor-22s in its option mounts: one forward, one in each wing (arcs from R8.R3).", []],"SG5.0":["Duel with a Pirate Raider", "The Duel (SG1.0) with an Orion Raider: the police ship in 0730 heading A and the pirate CR in 4203 heading E, speed 15, Weapon Status II (SG5.2). The book calls for a floating map where any unit may disengage in any direction (SG5.41) and the Modified Victory Conditions (S2.2). This game uses the Duel's edge rule and standard scoring. Captain's Basic Set page 211.", ["S2.2","SG1.0"]],"G8.1":["Transporters","Transporters move personnel between ships. They can only be used during the Marines Activity step of an impulse and need lock-on to both ends."],"G8.13":["Transporter energy","One unit of energy operates up to five of a ship's transporters."],"G8.112":["Transporter use","Each transporter can be used once a turn, and not twice within a quarter turn across two consecutive turns."],"G8.14":["Transporter range","The maximum range of transporters is five hexes."],"G8.17":["Fire control","The ship operating the transporter must have lock-on to both ends of the transfer. Ships using erratic maneuvering cannot use transporters."],"G8.21":["Blockage","Transporters will not function through shields."],"G8.22":["Dropping shields","A ship may voluntarily drop a specific shield to use transporters; it stays down for at least one-quarter of a turn."],"G8.23":["General reinforcement","General shield reinforcement blocks transporters even through dropped or destroyed shields. A blocked attempt costs nothing but uses the transporter for the turn."],"G8.31":["Combat rate","Sending boarding parties into a combat situation, each transporter can transport only one boarding party."],"G8.11":["Transporting","Transporters pick up or deliver people at a location, between ships and other objects."],"D7.3":["Boarding party combat","Boarding parties beamed onto an enemy ship fight its defenders."],"D7.31":["Combat","If boarding parties are transported onto an enemy ship, combat occurs between them and the ship's defenders."],"D7.32":["Sequence","Parties can arrive in any impulse, but combat is resolved at the end of the turn in the Final Activity Phase."],"D7.41":["Step 1","Each side counts its boarding parties on the ship under attack."],"D7.42":["Step 2","Each side rolls one die per group of ten parties and reads the casualty points it scores off the Marine Casualty Resolution Table."],"D7.421":["Marine casualty table","The table is read by die roll and number of boarding parties (1 to 10 per group)."],"D7.422":["Klingon security","Klingon marines on Klingon ships add one to the die roll for each undestroyed and uncaptured security station, up to +2."],"D7.44":["Step 4","Each side removes boarding parties equal to the casualties the other produced."],"D7.36":["Control","The capture of all undestroyed control rooms results in the capture of the ship."],"D7.361":["Giving up control rooms","The defender may give up control rooms instead of boarding party casualties."],"D7.362":["Control room casualties","A control room counts as two casualty points (three for a security station) and is captured, not destroyed."],"D7.50":["How to capture","A starship is captured when all undestroyed control rooms (including security stations) have been captured."],"G5.3":["Probes as emergency weapons","A probe launcher can be armed and fired as an anti-matter bomb only if the ship is crippled, the enemy outnumbers it by 50%, the scenario says so, all players agree, or it has a legendary captain or outstanding crew.",["G5.31","G5.32","G5.33","G5.34"]],"G5.31":["Probe combat","An anti-matter probe has a warhead of eight and is a direct-fire weapon with no overload and no feedback. ECM shifts apply."],"G5.32":["Probe arming","Two units of warp energy on two consecutive turns; it must be launched on the second turn and cannot be held."],"G5.33":["Probe firing arc","An anti-matter probe fires only along the row of hexes directly ahead of the ship. Exceptions: a probe on a base can fire in any direction, and ships using Directed Turn Modes (C3.8) have alternative arcs; neither applies in a Duel."],"G5.34":["Probe hit","Roll one die: a hit if the result is at least the range to the target. The probe can be fired at range zero."],"G5.35":["Probe functions","There is no overload, mine or proximity function."],"G5.11":["Probe range","Probes have a maximum range of six hexes."],"G5.12":["Probe ammunition","A probe launcher carries five probes unless stated otherwise."],"G7.4":["Tractoring a starship","A ship in your hex or an adjacent one can be tractored if you have an operable beam, lock-on and allocated power. The held ship may apply negative tractor, and the link strength must exceed it.",["G7.41","G7.42"]],"G7.41":["Tractor conditions","Adjacent or same hex, an operable beam, lock-on, and allocated power. The effective tractor strength must exceed (not equal) the negative tractor applied."],"G7.42":["Tractor auctions","A link made in a prior turn is auctioned at the start of each turn: the held ship announces negative tractor and the holder must exceed it or the link breaks."],"G7.35":["Negative tractor","Energy applied to negative tractor cancels one point of tractor strength each. It lasts the whole turn, needs no beam box and blocks friendly links as well."],"G7.352":["Negative tractor strength","Each point of negative tractor cancels one point of effective tractor power."],"G7.4221":["Losing an auction","If the tractoring ship loses, the power it committed is lost."],"G7.414":["Auction on link","If a link is made during a turn, an immediate auction is held using reserve and allocated power."],"G7.32":["Towing","Linked objects follow the towing ship on a parallel course and the movement cost per hex is the cost of all ships involved. Simplification here: the held ship does not use its own engines or turn."],"G7.122":["Breaking a link","A link can be broken by negative tractor, destruction of the beam, breakdown, disengagement and other means."],"G7.273":["Map edge","A tractor cannot be used to force a ship off a fixed map."],"G7.5":["Tractoring drones","A ship can capture an enemy drone or shuttle with a powered tractor beam if it is in the same or an adjacent hex and the ship has lock-on.",["G7.51","G7.521"]],"G7.51":["Capture conditions","The ship must have an operable tractor beam and lock-on, the unit must be in the same or an adjacent hex, and one point of tractor energy must be allocated."],"G7.521":["Held until fuel is exhausted","A drone that is tractored and held until its endurance runs out is removed from play."],"G7.11":["Tractor boxes","Each TRAC box on the SSD is one tractor beam."],"G7.12":["Tractor activation","Tractor beams are activated in the Operate Tractors step of an impulse."],"G7.124":["Tractors across turns","A tractor link can continue into the next turn only if new power is applied."],"G7.13":["Tractor use","Each beam can be used once a turn, and not again within eight impulses of its link being released or broken, even across a turn boundary."],"G7.15":["Tractor energy","One unit of energy operates each tractor beam."],"G7.26":["Seeking weapons","Plasma torpedoes cannot be held in a tractor beam."],"G7.33":["Releasing a tractor","A tractor beam can be released by its owner."],"FP8.0":["Plasma bolts","A plasma launcher can be fired as a direct-fire weapon instead of a seeking torpedo. It is less efficient but cannot be distracted or shot down.",["FP8.3","FP8.4"]],"FP8.3":["Bolt operation","A plasma bolt is a direct-fire weapon: declared and resolved with direct fire, with electronic warfare working as for any direct-fire weapon, and no overload.",["FP8.31","FP8.32","FP8.33","FP8.34","FP8.36"]],"FP8.31":["Bolt as direct fire","Resolved in the direct-fire stage. It cannot be distracted as seeking weapons can, or intercepted."],"FP8.32":["Bolt and EW","Net ECM shifts are added to the die roll. Bolts do not have the built-in ECCM of seeking torpedoes."],"FP8.33":["Bolt procedure","The launcher must be fully charged. The choice between seeking torpedo and bolt is made at the instant of firing."],"FP8.34":["No overload","There is no overload function for a plasma bolt."],"FP8.36":["Bolt feedback","If fired in the same hex as the target, the firing ship suffers feedback equal to 25% of the normal warhead."],"FP8.4":["Bolt resolution","Hit chance depends on effective range; damage is half the warhead of the corresponding torpedo at the true range.",["FP8.42","FP8.43"]],"FP8.42":["Bolt hit chance","Range 0-5 hits on 1-4; 6-10 on 1-3; 11-20 on 1-2; 21-31 on 1."],"FP8.43":["Bolt damage","Half the warhead strength of the corresponding torpedo at the true range; keep fractions through the calculation, then drop them. Bolts cannot be combined to avoid losing fractions."],"FP6.1":["Pseudo-plasma torpedo","A fake plasma torpedo. Every plasma launcher has one in a special tube. It can be launched for no energy at any time a real torpedo could be, whether or not a real one is armed.",["FP6.12","FP6.2","FP6.3"]],"FP6.12":["PPT launch","Launched for no energy cost at any point in the Sequence of Play and under any conditions at which a real torpedo could be fired."],"FP6.2":["PPT firing rate","One PPT per launcher per scenario, never in the same impulse as a real torpedo from the same launcher, and simulating the largest non-enveloping torpedo the launcher can fire.",["FP6.21","FP6.22","FP6.23"]],"FP6.21":["PPT firing limit","Only one PPT may be fired per launcher in a scenario."],"FP6.22":["Simultaneous launch","A PPT may not be fired in the same impulse as a real torpedo from the same launcher."],"FP6.23":["PPT type","A PPT can only simulate the largest non-enveloping torpedo its launcher can launch."],"FP6.3":["PPT operation","It moves, follows its target, absorbs phaser damage and behaves as a plasma torpedo in every way, but on reaching its target it simply disintegrates and does no damage.",["FP6.31"]],"FP6.31":["PPT impact","On impact it is revealed as a fake and no damage is scored."],"FP5.1":["Enveloping plasma torpedo","Plasma-armed ships can fire enveloping plasma torpedoes (EPTs) from type-G, S and R launchers instead of a normal torpedo. The firing ship needs a lock-on to the target at launch.",["FP5.2","FP5.3"]],"FP5.2":["Arming an EPT","Armed like a normal torpedo, but it needs twice the normal energy on the final turn of arming, which must be the turn of launch."],"FP5.21":["EPT strength","The warhead of an EPT is double that of the normal torpedo."],"FP5.22":["No holding","An EPT cannot be held."],"FP5.3":["EPT impact","The warhead, less general reinforcement, is divided by six; each shield takes that many points. The remainder goes one point each to shields of the defender's choice.",["FP5.31","FP5.32"]],"FP5.31":["EPT damage division","Subtract general reinforcement, divide by six rounding down, apply to every shield; the remaining points go one per shield on shields of the defender's choice."],"FP5.32":["EPT volley","Penetrating hits from all six shields are one volley."],"FP5.34":["EPT versus no shields","If the target has no shields, the whole warhead is a single volley."],"FP5.35":["General reinforcement","General reinforcement is subtracted from the EPT warhead before it is divided."],"E5.41":["Dogfight drones","Ships may load type-VI drones in the ADD launcher in place of anti-drones. They can only be launched at a target within six hexes of the firing ship."],"E5.42":["Firing dogfight drones","One type-VI drone per turn from each ADD; no type-VI and ADD fire in the same turn; eight impulses between switching modes."],"E5.43":["EM restriction","ADD racks cannot launch type-VI drones while the ship is doing erratic maneuvering."],"FD2.54":["Limited damage","Dogfight drones score two points of damage on a ship (size class 4 or larger)."],"FD2.55":["Dogfight range","Type-VI drones have a maximum range of twelve hexes."],"FD3.1":["Type-A drone rack","Four spaces of drones, one launch a turn, and not within 8 impulses of the previous launch. The standard rack."],"FD3.2":["Type-B drone rack","Six spaces of drones, one launch a turn. Most type-A racks were upgraded to B or C in Y175."],"FD3.3":["Type-C drone rack","Rapid fire: two launches a turn, at least twelve impulses apart even across turns. Four spaces of drones."],"E1.6":["Narrow salvoes","Direct-fire weapons of one type fired by one ship in one impulse at one target can be fired as a narrow salvo: one die roll is used for every weapon, so they all hit or all miss. Different phaser types can be combined; proximity and normal photons cannot. Seeking weapons cannot."],"E1.62":["Narrow salvo procedure","Resolve the first weapon normally; the others use the same die roll."],"E4.3":["Proximity fuse","A photon torpedo can carry a proximity fuse: it automatically misses at true range under 9, 2 is subtracted from the hit die roll at 9 or more, and its warhead is 4 instead of 8. The fuse is chosen in Energy Allocation and a proximity torpedo cannot be overloaded.",["E4.31","E4.32","E4.33","E4.34"]],"E4.31":["Proximity records","The proximity fuse is chosen when the second arming turn is recorded or when holding energy is paid, and can only be fitted or removed in Energy Allocation. It costs nothing."],"E4.32":["Proximity range and to-hit","Automatic miss at true range under nine hexes. At nine or more, subtract two from the die roll."],"E4.33":["Proximity warhead","The warhead of a proximity-fused torpedo is 4."],"E4.34":["Changing fuses","Fuses can be switched in Energy Allocation at no cost, but a proximity torpedo cannot be overloaded, and an overloaded one cannot be changed to proximity."],"D3.6":["Leaky shields","Optional. Every fourth (or sixth, or tenth) hit on a shield leaks through as an internal damage point instead of marking a shield box. Leaked and excess points in one damage step form a single volley.",["D3.61"]],"D3.61":["Leaky shields procedure","Allow every fourth (optionally sixth or tenth) hit to penetrate. All damage that penetrates a shield in one damage step of one impulse is one volley."],"C3.5":["Reversing direction","A ship may move backwards, turning exactly as it does going forward. The direction is set in Energy Allocation and cannot change during the turn. Before reversing it must pay braking energy equal to its speed last turn (warp, with at most one point from impulse); it then accelerates from zero in the opposite direction.",["C3.52","C3.6"]],"C3.51":["Mixed direction","A ship may not mix forward and reverse movement in a single turn."],"C3.52":["Braking energy","To reverse, a ship pays braking energy equal to last turn's speed (cost of movement, not hexes). Braking brings it to speed zero, so it can then accelerate by up to 10 in the new direction. Braking stops a positron flywheel."],"C3.6":["Quick reverse","A ship short of the full braking energy may try a quick reverse: it rolls one die on impulse 1, and if the roll is no more than the shortage (in movement points) it suffers a breakdown. No quick reverse within 1/4 turn (8 impulses) of an HET or another quick reverse.",["C3.5","C3.62"]],"C3.61":["Quick reverse roll","Roll a die in the movement segment of impulse 1. A result less than or equal to the shortage of braking energy is a breakdown; otherwise the ship has reversed."],"C3.62":["Quick reverse limit","A ship cannot quick-reverse within eight impulses of an HET or another quick reverse."],"C12.10":["Plotting speed changes","The ship plots, in Energy Allocation, the impulses where its speed changes. The new speed is announced one impulse earlier and the ship moves at it from the impulse given."],"C12.12":["Legal speed plot","Every speed change must be plotted during Energy Allocation and the plot must obey the limits for the number, spacing and size of changes."],"C12.2":["Energy cost of changing speed","The movement energy for the turn is the total number of hexes moved (times the movement cost), counted from the impulse chart for each speed."],"C12.21":["Total cost","Total hexes moved, adjusted by the movement cost, must be paid for."],"C12.311":["Number of changes","No more than four speed changes in one turn."],"C12.312":["Spacing","No speed change within eight impulses (1/4 turn) of another."],"C12.313":["Earliest and latest","A speed change takes effect no earlier than impulse 4 and no later than impulse 28."],"C12.32":["Deceleration","A ship cannot slow by more than half its current speed in one change (rounded up), or by 4 if it is moving at under speed 8."],"C12.33":["Adjustment limits","The lowest speed during the previous 32 impulses sets the highest speed the ship can accelerate to (lowest speed plus the greater of itself or 10)."],"C12.36":["Announcement","The new speed is announced on the impulse before it takes effect."],"C12.132":["Other speed changes","Changes caused by emergency deceleration, breakdown, damage balance and the like cancel a plotted speed change."],"D8.1":["Critical hit roll","Optional. If 20 or more damage points (including those absorbed by shields and reinforcement) hit one shield in a single impulse, the ship rolls two dice for a critical hit. Only one such roll is made per turn."],"D8.2":["Critical hit effects","2 active fire control fails (passive fire control), 3 battery failure, 4 transporters, 5 labs, 6-8 nothing, 9 tractors, 10 shuttle bay jammed, 11 maneuver restricted (speed 8 max, no HET or EM, turn mode +1), 12 warp controls damaged (the ship stops; half the warp output is usable for other purposes). A critical hit never destroys a system; it only stops it working until repaired.",["D8.1","D8.3","D19.1"]],"D8.23":["Warp critical hit","The ship stops moving immediately and cannot use warp for movement (half of the warp output can still be used for other purposes, D8.2). Impulse power allocated to movement may be used for one tactical maneuver for the rest of the turn, and the energy plan is adjusted at once by D22.0."],"D8.3":["Repairing critical hits","At the end of each turn damage control may try to repair one critical hit: roll one die, 1-4 repairs it, 5-6 does not. Later attempts on the same hit subtract 1 (second) or 2 (third and later) from the die.",["D8.2"]],"D8.31":["Repair procedure","Only one critical hit may be worked on per turn. The first attempt is at the end of the turn the hit happened."],"D19.1":["Passive fire control","With no active fire control the ship has no lock-on, so effective range is double the true range. It cannot use ECCM, cannot guide or launch seeking weapons, cannot fire at targets more than 5 hexes away (D19.23), and does not work while using erratic maneuvers (D19.25)."],"D19.23":["Passive fire control range","Direct-fire weapons can only be fired at targets within five hexes."],"D22.1":["Energy balance due to damage","Optional. When power boxes are destroyed mid-turn, power already spent is assumed to have come from them. If what is still needed (energy for items in use plus planned movement, HETs and EM) is more than what is left, the ship gives up energy."],"D22.3":["General power shortage","Remaining output is compared with the energy required for operating systems and available (unspent) allocations."],"D22.4":["Resolving a shortage","In order: reserve power (batteries), cancel unmade HETs, EM and tactical maneuvers, then slow the ship by up to half its speed (a smaller cut if it is enough), then give up further energy."],"D22.433":["Speed reduction","A ship that loses power slows down by up to 50% of its speed, or by less if that is enough. A ship that is trying to disengage by acceleration only drops to its new maximum speed."],"E2.251":["Low-power phaser fire","Any Ph-1 or Ph-2 can be fired as a Ph-3 (the Ph-3 damage table, range 15 or less) by spending only half a point of capacitor energy instead of a full point."],"S2.2":["Victory points","Each side scores a percentage of the enemy ship's BPV, only the greatest one: 10% for any internal damage, 25% for forcing it to disengage, 50% for crippling it, 100% for destroying it. The side with the lower total BPV also scores the difference unless it leaves by the end of turn 2."],"S2.3":["Levels of victory","Divide your score by the enemy's: 500%+ astounding, 300-499 decisive, 200-299 substantive, 150-199 tactical, 110-149 marginal victory, 91-109 draw, then marginal, tactical, brutal, crushing and devastating defeats below that."],"S2.41":["Crippled ship","A ship is crippled when any one is true: A) 10% or less of its warp boxes remain, B) half or more of its interior boxes are destroyed (not shields, armor, sensor, scanner or damage control), C) any excess damage is scored, D) all control spaces are destroyed, E) all weapons are destroyed."],"C6.521":["Nimble ships and HETs","A nimble ship subtracts two from the breakdown die roll of its first and second HET each scenario (a ship that is crippled gets no adjustment, C6.522)."],"E1.5":["Firing rates","No weapon may be fired twice within one quarter of a turn (eight impulses), even across the turn boundary. A weapon never fires more than once a turn."],"E1.7":["Small targets","Against direct fire, a nimble ship counts as having natural ECM: none out to range 14, +2 at 15 to 29, +4 at 30 or more (fighters and drones: 0-9, 10-19, 20+; shuttles and heavy fighters: 0-11, 12-24, 25+). Based on effective range. This is not cumulative with erratic maneuvering; the target uses whichever is better."],"SG1.41":["Disengaging at the edge of the map","The Duel uses a floating map. A ship may only leave the map by its own authorized edges: the Federation-side ship by the 42xx or xx01 edges, the Klingon-side ship by the 01xx or xx30 edges. A ship that leaves in any other direction is considered destroyed. Here the ship starting in 4203 uses the east and north edges, the one in 0730 the west and south edges."],"C5.1":["Sublight tactical maneuvers","A ship that stays in its hex can make one 60° turn on any impulse except the first, once a turn, by spending impulse power."],"C5.11":["Sublight maneuver procedure","The player announces the turn on the impulse he wants it, any impulse except #1. The ship stays in its hex and changes facing by one hexside."],"C5.12":["Impulse power restriction","The maneuver costs one point of impulse power, and the ship cannot move on impulse power during that turn."],"C5.31":["Warp and sublight maneuvers","A ship may use warp and sublight tactical maneuvers in the same turn but not in the same impulse. Five in total is the most in a turn (C5.33)."],"C9.0":["Positron flywheel (optional)","The flywheel stores momentum: a ship uses the fastest speed of its previous four turns as the base for its acceleration limit."],"C9.11":["Flywheel procedure","Speeds 21, 4, 4, 8 on turns 1 to 4 let a ship use 21 as its base on turn 5: it could accelerate to 42, but 31 is the maximum allowed."],"C9.231":["Emergency deceleration and the flywheel","Emergency deceleration stops the flywheel, so earlier speeds can no longer be used."],"C9.234":["Breakdown and the flywheel","A breakdown stops the flywheel and speeds before it cannot be used. A second breakdown destroys the flywheel."],"C9.3":["Flywheel cost","A BPV surcharge of 50% of the ship's basic BPV, rounded up."],"C10.0":["Erratic maneuvering (optional)","Minor but sharp and random course changes around a base course to reduce the chance of being hit."],"C10.11":["Cost of erratic maneuvers","One turn costs the energy of moving six hexes, in addition to normal movement (three hexes for a nimble ship). It must come from warp or impulse engines (C10.111)."],"C10.311":["Starting EM","A ship that has paid for EM may announce on any impulse that it begins at the end of that impulse. It can start once per turn."],"C10.32":["Stopping EM","A ship may stop on any impulse; the energy is lost and it cannot restart that turn."],"C10.41":["ECM effect","Using EM has the effect of four points of ECM, which ECCM can offset. It counts as a natural source."],"C10.414":["EM and your own fire","The same ECM applies to direct-fire weapons fired by the EM ship, because the maneuvers are random and cannot be predicted even by the ship."],"C10.511":["EM restrictions","A ship using EM cannot launch drones, shuttles, fighters, probes or plasma torpedoes, nor guide seeking weapons."],"C10.55":["EM and maneuver","A ship using EM has its turn mode increased by one hex and adds one to HET die rolls. Nimble ships are exempt."],"C8.23":["Erratic maneuvers and deceleration","A ship using erratic maneuvering that makes an emergency deceleration stops the erratic maneuvering."],"D6.5":["Ubitron interface modules (UIM)","A UIM improves disruptor hit chances: standard shots at effective range 16 to 22 are resolved as range 15, and overloads at 3 to 8 as range 1 to 2. It needs fire control and lock-on, and every impulse it is used there is a 1 or 2 on a die that burns it out. Disruptors fired under a burnt-out UIM are locked out for 32 impulses and a spare module takes 8 impulses to come on line. In this game the UIM is used automatically whenever it helps.",["D6.52","D6.54"],"UIM: normal disruptors firing at effective ranges of sixteen to 22 are resolved as if fired at fifteen. A 1 or 2 breaks it down. The disruptors may not fire for 32 impulses. A backup module cannot be used for 1/4 turn."],"E3.62":["DERFACS","Disruptor fire at effective range 23 to 30 is resolved as if the range were 22 (hit chance only, damage stays that of the true range). It cannot break down.",[],"DERFACS: All disruptor fire at effective ranges of 23-30 is resolved as if the effective range was 22. DERFACS cannot break down."],"E5.0":["Anti-drones (ADD)","An ADD rack fires one anti-drone per impulse at a drone within 3 hexes: it hits automatically at range 0, on 1-2 at range 1, 1-3 at range 2 and 1-4 at range 3, never at 4 or more (the scanner factor is added to the die roll; erratic maneuvering adds one). A hit destroys the drone. A rack holds 6 rounds, 12 after the Y175 refit. It needs lock-on and ignores EW. In this game it fires automatically.",["E5.21"],"ADD: one ADD per impulse; a hit automatically destroys the drone; lock-on required; EW ignored."],"H4.3":["AWR (advanced warp reactors)","An AWR replaces an APR. Its power counts like APR power, except that it cannot be used for movement, HETs or erratic manoeuvres. It can pay for photon torpedo arming and every other use.",["H4.2"],"AWR POWER: Power from AWRs cannot be used for movement, HET or EM, but can be used for other warp power uses."],"A4.1":["First Cadet scenario","The simplest scenario in the book: two ships fight with phasers only. It lists the rules to read first and is the basis for the \"Fly straight\" style of play.",["B2.3","B3.1","C2.21"],"FIRST CADET SCENARIO: COMBAT WITH PHASERS Read the following rules: (B2.1), (B2.3), (B3.0), (C1.1), (C1.2), (C1.31), (C1.4), (C2.0), (C3.0),"],"B2.3":["Sequence of Play","Each turn has: 1 Energy Allocation, 2 Speed Determination, 3 Sensor Lock-on, 4 32 Impulse Procedures (movement, seeking weapons, fire), 5 End of turn record keeping. This game does steps 1 and 2 together, then steps through the impulses, pausing for your fire decisions.",["B3.1","C1.43","D6.11"],"SEQUENCE OF PLAY EXPLANATION This section describes the basic operation of the Sequence of Play and the turn procedure. Note that a more detailed listing of all actions that can be taken during the Sequence of Play, in the specific order in which they occur, is given in Annex #2. 1. ENERGY ALLOCATION PHASE: Each player, for each ship that he controls, fills out the next available column on the Energy Allocation Form for that ship. Refer to the Energy Allocation Rules"],"B3.1":["Energy Allocation","Each turn, secretly, you spend power: life support, fire control, electronic warfare, phaser capacitor, weapons arming and holding, batteries, shields, reinforcement, shield repair, High Energy Turns, then movement. Warp power comes from the engine boxes still alive, impulse and APR power from theirs. Unspent warp power is lost; the allocation must be paid exactly.",["B3.21","C2.21","D3.3","H7.41"],"PROCEDURE Energy is calculated and allocated on an Energy Allocation Form. There are several of these included with the game. Players should use plastic page protectors and grease pencils or photocopy extra copies of this form for use in the game. Packages with extra copies of the EAF are available for sale in some hobby stores. Each turn during the Energy Allocation Phase, each player, for every starship he controls, must fill out the next column of his EAF. Special Energy Allocation Forms for use by specialized units are provided in Module R1. The Andromedans in Module C2 have their own special Energy Allocation Form. Specific instructions for filling out the form are given on a line by line basis, as follows: 1. WARP ENGINES: This is the amount of warp engine power available. Simply count the unchecked warp engine boxes on the ship’s SSD. …"],"B3.21":["Fractions of energy","Fractional energy costs (for example half a point for a phaser-3 shot or a ship that moves at 2/3 point per hex) may be combined before rounding, so a ship pays the total, rounded up.",["C2.12","H6.0"],"FRACTIONS: Fractions of energy points used for various systems may be combined. For example, a Klingon C8 dreadnought uses 1-1/2 energy points to move one hex and uses 1/2 of an energy point to fire a phaser-3. Therefore, two energy points (1-1/2 + 1/2 = 2) would be needed to perform these two functions, but without the fractional accounting system, three points would be required since all energy expenditures are rounded up."],"C1.43":["Impulse movement","Every unit moves in a proportional pattern across the 32 impulses of a turn using the Impulse Chart. A ship at speed 16 moves on half the impulses, a ship at 32 on all of them. Drones and plasma torpedoes use the same chart.",["C2.12","F2.11"],"MOVEMENT PROCEDURE: During the Impulse Procedure, the various units on the map (including shuttles, drones, and plasma torpedoes) are moved by a proportional movement system. In simple terms, this means that if unit “A” is moving at ten hexes per turn, unit “B” is moving at five hexes per turn, and the turn is divided into a number of impulses (with no unit moving more than one hex per impulse), then unit “A” will move during twice as many of those impulses as unit “B” and will, of course, move twice as far. A more detailed version of the procedure is included in the Explanation of the Sequence of Play (B2.3)."],"C2.12":["Warp movement points","Warp energy allocated to movement is divided by the ship's movement cost per hex (shown on the SSD). Impulse power gives 1 hex per point. The total, rounded up, is the ship's speed for the turn.",["B3.21","C2.21"],"WARP MOVEMENT POINTS: Warp movement points are generated by taking the number of warp engine energy points (those which have been allocated for movement) and dividing this number by the movement cost of the ship. This movement cost is shown on the SSD sheets and on the MASTER SHIP CHART (Annex #3). Each resulting warp movement point yields one hex of movement."],"C2.21":["Maximum speed change","A ship may raise its speed by no more than its previous speed or ten, whichever is greater. A ship stopped last turn can reach speed 10, a ship at 20 can reach 40 (limited to 31 by the Impulse Chart).",["C2.12","B3.1"],"MAXIMUM INCREASE: When allocating energy to movement, the player may increase the ship’s practical speed by a number which is equal to the previous turn’s speed, or ten, whichever is greater. This includes warp and impulse power. EXAMPLE: If speed on Turn #5 was three, then it could be increased to no more than thirteen on Turn #6 (thirteen is ten more than three). If speed is increased to thirteen on Turn #6, then on Turn #7 speed could be increased to 26 (26 is double thirteen). EXCEPTIONS: There are exceptions to this noted in the specific rule for each ship. For example: freighters (R1.5) and (R1.6), X-ships"],"C3.1":["Turning","A ship turns 60 degrees at the start of an impulse, before it moves. Its Turn Mode controls how many hexes it must move straight between turns.",["C3.2","C3.41"],"TURNING The actual act of turning the unit by 60° is done at the start of a given impulse (immediately before moving into the next hex) and NOT at the end of the impulse (after entering a given hex). A unit’s Turn Mode regulates how often a 60° turn can be made."],"C3.2":["Turn Mode","Ships have a turn mode letter A to E; at higher speeds the number of hexes needed between turns increases. Smaller, slower-to-turn ships should plan turns before they close.",["C3.1","C3.41"],"DEFINITION OF TURN MODE"],"C3.41":["Turn mode carry-over","Hexes moved straight at the end of one turn count towards the next turn. A ship that has not completed its turn mode on one turn must finish it on the next before turning, unless it makes an HET or changes speed.",["C3.1"],"CARRYOVER: Turn Mode restrictions carry over from turn to turn. For example, a unit with a Turn Mode of four that moves 6A, 1B (six hexes in direction A followed by one in direction B) on a given turn must move B3 (three hexes in direction B) on the next turn before making a turn to C or A (assuming no HET or change of speed)."],"C6.0":["High Energy Turns (HET)","A ship can turn to face any adjacent hex, ignoring turn mode, by paying energy and risking a breakdown. See the sub-rules for procedure, cost and breakdown.",["C6.1","C6.21","C6.51"],"and Tactical Maneuvers (C5.0) can also produce a facing change and hence, in effect, a turn. See (C4.4) for an illustrated example."],"C6.1":["HET procedure","An HET is plotted for a specific impulse and turns the ship to face any adjacent hex regardless of its turn mode. It cannot be made on impulse 1 and not within a quarter turn (8 impulses) of a previous HET.",["C6.21","C6.36","C6.37"],"PROCEDURE The effect of a High Energy Turn is to, at the point for which it is plotted, turn the ship TO FACE ANY ADJACENT HEX regardless of whether or not it has satisfied its Turn Mode. [See (C6.39) for a limitation.] High Energy Turns may be made during the Movement Segment of any impulse, regardless of whether or not the ship is scheduled to move, within the restrictions of (C6.3), and subject to a possible breakdown (C6.5)."],"C6.21":["HET cost","An HET costs warp energy equal to five hexes of movement (the number on the SSD \"HET cost\"), paid in addition to normal movement, and spent during energy allocation whether or not the HET is used.",["C6.1","B3.1"],"COST: Each High Energy Turn requires warp energy equal to five hexes of movement. The cost must be paid in addition to any energy used for movement. Exceptions: Poor Crews (G21.122), Outstanding Crews (G21.222)."],"C6.32":["HET and turn mode","After an HET, turn mode starts again from zero from the hex where the HET was made. A ship need not have completed its turn mode to make an HET.",["C6.1","C3.41"],"TURN MODE: After a High Energy Turn, Turn Mode calculations (for normal turns) and sideslips must begin again, starting in the hex in which the HET was made (i.e., an HET resets the turn and sideslip modes to zero). A ship need not have fulfilled its Turn Mode to make an HET. See (C3.45)."],"C6.36":["HET spacing","An HET cannot be made within a quarter turn (8 impulses) of an earlier HET.",["C6.1"],"SEQUENCE: An HET may not be performed within 1/4 turn of a previous HET or Quick Reverse; see (C3.62)."],"C6.37":["HET not allowed","No HET on the first impulse of a turn, while docked, uncontrolled, and in a few other special cases.",["C6.1"],"HET NOT ALLOWED: No unit may make an HET on the first impulse of the turn, while docked (C13.924), while docking (C13.16), while undocking (C13.23), or while part of a pinwheel (C14.12), or if uncontrolled (G2.212). Exception (F2.135)."],"C6.51":["Breakdown roll","Each HET carries a risk of breakdown. Roll one die; if the result is equal to or above the ship's breakdown rating (for example 5-6 means 5 or 6) the ship breaks down. The rating gets worse after each breakdown.",["C6.54","C6.544"],"PROCEDURE: Whenever a ship performs an HET, roll a single die to determine if the ship has suffered a breakdown. If the die roll result, after adding any adjustments, is equal to or greater than the numbers specified in the ship’s breakdown rating, the ship suffers a breakdown."],"C6.54":["Effects of breakdown","A breakdown stops the ship, points it in a random direction, wastes all movement energy for the turn, destroys some warp boxes, causes two internal damage points, and prevents movement, HETs and weapons fire for 16 impulses.",["C6.541","C6.5422","C6.5423","C6.5471"],"EFFECTS OF BREAKDOWN: Whenever a ship suffers a breakdown, it suffers the following effects:"],"C6.541":["Breakdown: stop and no moves","The ship immediately stops, faces a random direction, loses all allocated movement energy and may not move for 16 impulses after the breakdown.",["C6.54"],"The ship immediately stops [unless it “tumbles” (C6.55)]. Roll one die and face the ship in the resulting direction. All allocated movement energy (including EM, Tactical Maneuvers, and HETs) is lost. The ship may not move (including HETs and Tactical Maneuvers) for sixteen impulses after the impulse in which it broke down. This is known as the “post-breakdown period.” After this period, the ship could resume normal movement (e.g., it could do a Tactical Maneuver with reserve power)."],"C6.5422":["Breakdown: engine damage","Every fifth warp engine box is destroyed (rounded down), spread as evenly as possible across the engines.",["C6.54"],"Every fifth warp engine box is destroyed (round fractions down when calculating these losses). This is distributed as evenly as possible between the ship’s engines; any odd points are distributed by the owning player at his option."],"C6.5423":["Breakdown: internal damage","The ship takes two internal damage points, distributed on the Damage Allocation Chart as a single volley. Those points count toward the crew casualties of G9.21.",["C6.54","D4.2"],"The ship suffers two interior damage points, distributed randomly and immediately by the DAMAGE ALLOCATION CHART (D4.21). This damage (as well as that resulting from tumbling and pod separation) is resolved as a single volley; the phaser directional restriction (D4.321) does not apply."],"C6.544":["Breakdown rating worsens","Each breakdown reduces the breakdown rating by one for the rest of the scenario (for example 4-6 becomes 3-6), and nimble ships lose their nimble bonuses.",["C6.51"],"Each breakdown reduces the breakdown rating by one for the remainder of the scenario. For example, 4-6 becomes 3-6 (and possibly 2-6 and 1-6 later, although it can never get worse than 1-6). Nimble ships lose all benefits of that status (C11.2) for the remainder of the scenario after a breakdown."],"C6.5471":["Breakdown: no weapons","For 16 impulses after a breakdown the ship cannot fire any weapons.",["C6.54"],"No weapons (see Annex #7D) may be fired."],"D3.13":["Shield status","A shield is \"down\" when its boxes are all destroyed and \"dropped\" when the owner has switched it off. Both leave that facing open to damage.",["D3.3","D3.343"],"STATUS: Shields can be “down” [reduced to zero strength by damage (D3.21)] or “dropped” [voluntarily inactivated to facilitate the use of transporters (D3.5)]."],"D3.3":["Shield energy","Shields can run at minimum or full power, or be repaired. The cost depends on the ship's size class: for cruisers 1 for minimum and 2 for full. Minimum shields only protect the boxes if five or more remain on that shield.",["D3.32","D3.34","D3.341"],"ENERGY COST OF SHIELDS Shields can be operated at “minimum” level or at “full strength.” They can also be repaired. All of this requires varying amounts of energy depending on the ship. NOTE: As with most rules involving energy, the optional Energy Balance due to Damage (D22.0) rules could result in allocated power being cancelled in mid-turn. COMBAT — D"],"D3.32":["Shield cost by size class","Costs per turn, shown as minimum + extra for full: size 1 bases 2+5=7, size 2 dreadnoughts 1+3=4, size 3 cruisers 1+1=2, size 4 destroyers and frigates 0.5+0.5=1. Full shields cannot run without the minimum.",["D3.3"],"COST OF OPERATION: The cost to operate a ship’s shields is based on its size. The size class of each ship is shown on the MASTER SHIP CHART. The cost to operate shields (per turn) is shown below: SIZE CLASS MINIMUM FULL TOTAL 1 (Starbases) = 2 + 5 = 7 2 (Dreadnoughts) = 1 + 3 = 4 3 (Cruisers) = 1 + 1 = 2 4 (Destroyers) = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 5 (PFs) = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 6 (Shuttles) — Not Applicable — 7 (Seeking weapons) — Not Applicable — Note that all costs are given as Minimum+Full, and full shields cannot be operated without also operating minimum shields. For a ship to have all of the boxes on its SSD active, it pays the number in the “Total” column. Separated booms and saucers (G12.332) do not pay for shields."],"D3.34":["Reinforcement","Energy can be spent to absorb hits before any shield box is marked off. General reinforcement works as long as shields are at least at minimum level, even if no shield boxes remain. Ships that never had shields cannot use either kind.",["D9.21","D9.7"],"and repaired by Damage Control (D9.2). Shields can also be repaired by Continuous Damage Control (D9.7), but this would probably be a waste of resources. Shields can also be repaired by Repair systems (G17.32), but they cannot be repaired by Emergency Damage Repair (D14.23)."],"D3.341":["General reinforcement","Two energy points make one point of general reinforcement, which absorbs one point of damage from any direction before the shield boxes are hit. It lasts for the turn it was paid for.",["D3.3411","D3.342"],"can stop damage that would otherwise hit a down shield."],"D3.3411":["Reinforcement order","When damage is resolved, general reinforcement is used first, then specific reinforcement, and only then are shield boxes destroyed. The owner cannot change this order.",["D3.341","D3.342"],"When resolving damage, any points of operating general reinforcement must be used before using any points of specific reinforcement, and all reinforcement must be used before any SSD boxes are destroyed. This is NOT at the option of either player."],"D3.342":["Specific reinforcement","One energy point adds one extra box to a single named shield for this turn only. It is absorbed after general reinforcement and before the real boxes.",["D3.341","D3.3411"],"SPECIFIC REINFORCEMENT: Energy supplied for specific reinforcement adds a number of “extra” boxes (equal to the amount of energy applied) to that shield for the duration of the current turn."],"D3.343":["Down shields and reinforcement","A shield that is down or dropped cannot be given specific reinforcement, but general reinforcement still protects that direction while it lasts.",["D3.13","D3.341"],"DOWN SHIELDS: A shield that is down (or which has been dropped) cannot be reinforced, but general reinforcement would still block fire coming from that direction (for as long as it lasts). EXAMPLE: A given ship has a front shield consisting of twenty boxes. The ship has allocated ten points of energy to reinforce the front shield and six points to general reinforcement. At a given point in the turn, two damage points are scored on a different shield, eliminating two of the three points of general reinforcement. Later during the turn, fifteen points of damage is scored on the front shield. The first point is stopped by general reinforcement, the next ten by specific reinforcement, and the last four score “permanent” hits (i.e., they check off boxes which could be repaired later) on the shield, reducing it from twenty boxes to sixteen. …"],"D4.12":["Armor","Some ships (for example the Romulan Warbird and War Eagle) have armor boxes. Armor absorbs damage after the shields and before internal damage; each armor box takes one damage point and is destroyed.",["D4.13","D4.2"],"ARMOR: Some of the ships included in the game have armor installed in their hulls (specifically: starbases, base stations, battle stations, the Federation CL, the Romulan Warbird/War Eagle, and some others noted in their ship descriptions and/or on their SSDs). The term “armor” accounts for a variety of defensive systems, not simply thick metal plates. A series of internal energy dampers was used on early ships (before shields) and is treated as armor on those ships. Damage points which penetrate the shields strike the armor (each damage point destroying one armor box); after the armor is destroyed, the damage proceeds normally. The special starbase damage procedures (R1.1D) account for armor in a slightly different manner."],"D4.13":["Internal hits","Damage that gets through the shield and any armor is scored on interior systems, one point at a time, using the Damage Allocation Chart.",["D4.22","D4.12"],"INTERNAL HITS: Any hits which penetrate the shields and armor are distributed among the interior systems of the ship (causing considerable damage) by the Damage Allocation Procedure (D4.2) which uses the DAMAGE ALLOCATION CHART (D4.21). See (D4.23) for some exceptions."],"D4.2":["Damage allocation","Each point of internal damage rolls two dice on the Damage Allocation Chart. The result names a system (for example Phaser, Warp, Torp, Drone). Damage from one shield in one step is a single volley.",["D4.22","D4.222","D4.323"],"DAMAGE ALLOCATION PROCEDURE This procedure is used to distribute damage points which have penetrated to the interior of the ship. These points are distributed by the DAMAGE ALLOCATION CHART. For NVC see (D6.4)."],"D4.22":["Damage procedure","First work out how many damage points got past the shield, reinforcement and armor. Each point is allocated separately on the Damage Allocation Chart, in order, and all points from one shield in one step are resolved together as a volley.",["D4.222","D4.13"],"PROCEDURE: First determine the number of damage points which have penetrated the shield (including reinforcement) and armor (if any). Each internal damage point must be distributed individually. All damage points scored against a given shield in a given damage step must be resolved together and are collectively known as a “volley.” Note that fire from several directions which all strikes the same shield is still resolved as a single volley, see (D4.34). Fire from several directions that strikes different shields is resolved as separate volleys, i.e., all fire that strikes the #1 shield of a given ship on a given impulse is one volley. Fire on a given impulse that strikes the #1 shield and #2 shield would be resolved as two separate volleys under (D4.3). …"],"D4.222":["Reading the chart","Roll two dice and look at column A. The hit destroys one undamaged box of that system. If none are left, read the other columns in order (B, C and so on) until something can be hit. If nothing at all, the damage point is lost to the excess damage track.",["D4.22","D4.323"],"Look across (to the right of) the die roll result, and note the system listed in column “A.” Normally, the damage point in question is scored against one box of that type. Players must allocate EACH damage point of a given volley by this procedure. However, if there are no remaining boxes of that type on the target ship’s SSD (or never were any), move one column to the right and score the damage STAR FLEET BATTLES point against the system listed in column “B.” If there are no undestroyed boxes of the system type listed in column “B,” move on to column “C” and so on. See (D4.323) and Annex #7E."],"D4.321":["Directional phaser hits","A hit on phasers must destroy a phaser that could fire back along the direction the damage came from, if there is one.",["D4.222"],"PHASER DIRECTIONAL HITS: In the case of hits against phasers, the hit must be scored against a phaser that is capable of firing in the direction from which the volley came (i.e., through the shield in the case of seeking or enveloping weapons, or of firing at the firing unit in the case of direct-fire weapons). See also (D4.34) which says that if multiple units contribute to the same volley, you resolve them in the order of the amount of damage each scored, largest damage score to lowest, for phaser directional damage purposes. See (C6.5423) for another special case. See (C6.555) for additional damage that is part of the same volley. ONLY phasers use the “directional” rule for damage allocation. …"],"D4.323":["TORP and DRONE results","On the chart, \"TORP\" hits photon tubes, disruptors and plasma launchers; \"DRONE\" hits drone racks (and other special weapons). If the ship has none, the next column is used.",["D4.222"],"ALTERNATE HIT RESULTS: The result “TORP” on the DAMAGE ALLOCATION CHART is applied to disruptor bolt, photon torpedo, plasma-D racks, fusion beam, TR beam, and plasma torpedo boxes on the SSDs. The result “DRONE” on the DAC is applied to drone racks, PPDs, web casters, hellbores, ESGs, Andromedan PA panels, and anti-drones. There are some additional exceptions noted in various rules sections. See also Annex #7E."],"D6.11":["Lock-on","A unit needs a lock-on to its target to shoot at full effect. Lock-on is rolled at the start of each turn; here it is automatic unless sensors have been damaged.",["D6.123","D6.21"],"LOCK-ON: Units must have a lock-on to their target to fire their weapons with their full effect. Units roll for lock-on in the Sensor Lock-On Phase of each turn, and possibly at other times. If the sensors are damaged during a turn, the unit does not roll again for general lock-on until the Sensor Lock-On Phase of the next turn. However, rolls required during the turn against a specific unit (perhaps one emerging from behind a planet or from a cloak) would use the current sensor rating (which might have been reduced by damage during the turn). Roll a single die, and if the result is equal to or less than the sensor rating (the highest unchecked number on the sensor track of the SSD), the lock-on is achieved. Note that as almost all ships have a “6” in the first box of their sensor track (or an assumed rating of six), until damaged, sensor lock-on is automatic. …"],"D6.123":["No lock-on penalty","Without lock-on, the range to the target is doubled for weapons that need a lock. If the doubled range (plus the scanner adjustment) is beyond the weapon's maximum range it cannot fire.",["D6.11","D6.21"],"The firing range to all non-locked targets for other weapons is doubled. This doubling refers to the true range, not the range after being adjusted for scanners (D6.21). If the effective range (after doubling for no lock-on and adding the scanner factor) is beyond the maximum range of the weapon, the weapon cannot fire. See also"],"D6.21":["Scanner adjustment","Add the lowest undestroyed number on the scanner track to the range when firing, after any doubling for no lock-on. Scanner damage therefore makes your long-range fire worse.",["D6.123","E1.82"],"SCANNER ADJUSTMENT: The scanner adjustment factor is the lowest numbered undestroyed box on the scanner track. This is initially “0” for most ships. When computing firing range, always add the scanner adjustment factor to the actual range in hexes [which may be doubled due to failure to achieve sensor lock-on (D6.1)]. EXAMPLE: A Klingon D6 battlecruiser is having a rather difficult time with a Kzinti strike cruiser. The D6 has taken two hits on its sensors (reducing this factor from six to five) and three hits on its scanners (increasing this number from zero to three). The Klingon wishes to fire and rolls for sensor lock-on. He rolls a “6” and does not achieve lock-on. The true range is three hexes. This is doubled to six, and then the scanner adjustment factor of three is added to that, resulting in an effective range of nine. …"],"D6.3":["Electronic warfare","ECM adds to your own ship's defence and ECCM cancels the enemy's ECM. The net shift moves the die roll on weapon tables, and it is limited by your sensor track.",["D6.310","D6.34","E1.82"],"ELECTRONIC WARFARE (Some elements are Commander’s Level) X These rules simulate the use of Electronic Counter Measures (ECM) and Electronic Counter-Counter Measures (ECCM). These are collectively known as Electronic Warfare (EW). Some of these rules are used at all times as standard rules to explain such things as terrain effects (P0.0) and small target modifiers (E1.7). These “Standard” EW rules are marked as such in this section. It is not possible to play the game without them although in the simplest scenarios they will not be used. The Primary difference between the “Standard” and “Commander’s” EW systems is that ships can spend energy to gain EW points under the Commander’s system and cannot do so under the Standard system. Other differences include: USED under Standard System: Terrain (P0.0), Wild Weasels"],"D6.310":["Generating EW","Energy put into ECM and ECCM together cannot exceed the highest undestroyed number on the sensor track (usually six).",["D6.3","D6.312"],"GENERATION: Ships may use energy for ECM or ECCM. The total amount of energy put into (allocated for) ECM and ECCM combined cannot exceed the highest unchecked number on the sensor track (usually six). No more than this number of units of power can be allocated for EW during an Energy Allocation Phase. Additional power may be applied using reserve power during the turn; see (D6.312). Energy must be designated for use as ECM or ECCM at the time it is allocated (or when reserve power is committed)."],"D6.312":["EW from reserve power","Batteries can add ECM or ECCM at the moment of firing, within the same sensor limit.",["D6.310","H7.2"],"RESERVE POWER (H7.2) may be used to increase ECM or ECCM strength during the Fire Decision Step of any impulse, but not in excess of the limits stated for total power applied. In such cases, the EW calculations must be made again at the time such power is announced. A player could drop some active EW points (ECM or ECCM) and use reserve power to activate EW points of the other type. All ships have a number of “circuits” for EW equal to their sensor rating (which is usually six). Each can be used for ECM or ECCM, but not both at the same time. A given circuit can be changed from ECM to ECCM no more than once every quarter-turn, even if this creates a restriction which extends into the next turn. …"],"D6.34":["Net EW effect","Target ECM minus attacker ECCM gives the net EW. A net of 1-3 shifts the firing die roll by +1, 4-8 by +2, 9-15 by +3 (so a higher roll misses more often). Seeking weapons use a different table when they hit.",["D6.36","E1.82"],"CALCULATION OF EFFECT (Standard): To determine the net effect of ECM and ECCM, make the following calculation: STEP 1: Determine the ECM strength of the target unit (D6.314). This may be adjusted (usually increased) by various factors, including ECM drones (FD9.0), SWAC shuttles (J9.0), scouts (G24.0), or other means. Note that some units have an additional ECM strength built-in due to their design; see ship descriptions. Also note that certain objects, such as terrain (P0.0), can produce an ECM effect. STEP 2: Determine the ECCM strength of the firing unit. In the case of seeking weapons, this is the ECCM strength of the unit guiding the seeking weapon and/or the ECCM strength of the weapon itself (if any). Note that some units have an automatic built-in ECCM strength in addition to any power they apply. …"],"D6.36":["EW against seeking weapons","When a drone or plasma torpedo reaches its target the ECM shift is added to a die roll: 6 or less is full strength, 7-8 half, 9-10 a quarter, more is a miss.",["D6.34","F2.31"],"EFFECT ON SEEKING WEAPONS: In the case of seeking weapons (F0.0), the effect of ECM/ECCM is determined when the weapon reaches its target. The ECM shift is used to adjust the die roll on the table below:"],"D6.6":["Active fire control","Ships keep fire control active for one energy point. It is needed to shoot, launch seeking weapons and keep lock-on. Cloaked ships cannot use it.",["D6.11","F3.31"],"ACTIVE FIRE CONTROL Ships (including bases, PFs, and interceptors) continuously use their sensors to analyze the surrounding area and locate various items of use or interest, including potential threats and targets. This system is generally referred to as “fire control” but is used for much more than simply firing weapons. Non-ship units (e.g., fighters) use the same active fire control systems, but as they do not allocate power, the procedures are greatly simplified."],"D9.2":["Shield repair in combat","Damage control energy repairs shield boxes at the end of the turn.",["D9.21"],"REPAIRING SHIELDS IN COMBAT"],"D9.21":["Shield repair procedure","Allocate energy to damage control up to the highest number on the track. Each two energy points repair one shield box at the end of that turn; you name the shield in advance.",["D9.2","D3.34"],"PROCEDURE: During any turn, energy may be allocated to damage control up to the highest number on the track. For each two units of energy allocated to damage control, the damage scored to one shield box may be erased at the end of the turn. You must specify which shield you are repairing during the Energy Allocation Phase. If the DC rating is reduced during the turn, energy applied at the start still counts for that turn. EXAMPLE: A Federation heavy cruiser puts four points of power into damage repair for shields (the maximum it can due to its damage control rating of four) and, at the end of the turn in the Repair Stage of the Record Keeping Phase, two shield boxes are repaired. STAR FLEET BATTLES"],"D9.7":["Continuous damage repair","An optional rule where damage control repairs systems every turn. It is not in this game.",["D9.2"],"CONTINUOUS DAMAGE REPAIR"],"E1.82":["Die roll shifts","A die roll shift moves the firing roll towards a miss (higher) or hit (lower) on the weapon table. EW shifts and range shifts are added, then the damage is read from the table.",["D6.34","D6.21"],"EFFECT OF DIE ROLL SHIFTS: Die roll shifts are applied as follows:"],"E3.5":["Overloaded disruptors","A disruptor can be overloaded with extra energy for double damage at short range (up to 8) but with a smaller maximum range. It is spent whether or not it hits.",["E3.55","H7.54"],"Overloaded disruptors"],"E3.55":["Disruptor overload from reserve","Reserve power from batteries can overload a disruptor.",["E3.5","H7.54"],"RESERVE POWER can be used to overload a disruptor (H7.54)."],"E4.2":["Photon operation","Photon torpedoes are armed over two turns with two energy per turn, and then fired, held or discharged.",["E4.22","E4.41"],"OPERATIONS"],"E4.22":["Holding photons","A photon that has finished arming and is not fired this turn must be discharged or held for one energy point per tube (held tubes are ready the next turn).",["E4.2"],"HOLDING ARMED TORPEDOES: If the arming of a photon torpedo has been completed on a given turn, and the torpedo is not fired on that turn, then the ship must either discharge the weapon"],"E4.41":["Photon overloads","A photon torpedo can be overloaded up to 100% more (to eight points) with extra warp energy, for more damage at short range and a shorter maximum range.",["E4.411","E4.45"],"LEVELS OF OVERLOAD: Photon torpedoes can be overloaded by up to 100%."],"E4.411":["Photon overload energy","Extra warp energy of up to four points, in half-point steps, applied during the two arming turns (or later as reserve warp) overloads the torpedo.",["E4.41"],"During the arming process for a normal photon torpedo, a total of four units of warp energy is applied to charging the torpedo (two on each of two consecutive turns). If additional warp energy (up STAR FLEET BATTLES to four points in increments of one-half point) is applied during the two-turn arming process or while the torpedo is being held in the tube, this energy has the effect of overloading the torpedo. This overload energy may be applied during the first and/or second turn of arming and/or any later turn in which a loaded torpedo is held. Note that while overload energy (up to the maximum of four points) can be applied in the first turn of arming, this irrevocably commits the torpedo to be an overloaded torpedo (unless it is discharged). Discharged is defined in (E1.24). The table below shows some of the legal possibilities for arming and overloading photon torpedoes. …"],"E4.45":["Overload with reserve power","A fully armed photon can be overloaded when it is about to fire using reserve warp power (not other battery power).",["E4.41","H7.54"],"OVERLOADING WITH RESERVE POWER: A photon that has completed arming could be overloaded during the turn using reserve power (H7.54). However, this must be reserve warp energy (H7.48). This could be done at the point of firing."],"F1.22":["Launching seeking weapons","In the standard game drones and other seeking weapons can be launched during any impulse in the seeking weapons stage, not only at the start or end of the turn.",["F3.21","FD4.2"],"STANDARD GAME: In the Standard Game, either (or both) may be launched during the Seeking Weapons Stage of any impulse. Seeking weapons are NOT launched during the Initial or Final Activity Phases in the Standard Game."],"F2.11":["Seeking weapon speed","Each type of seeking weapon moves at a fixed speed (a drone at 8, 12 or 20 hexes a turn, a plasma torpedo at 32), using the Impulse Chart.",["C1.43"],"SPEED: Seeking weapons move with a fixed practical speed."],"F2.12":["Seeking weapon turn mode","Drones and plasma torpedoes may only turn after moving a set number of hexes since the last turn (a turn mode), shown with each weapon.",["F2.21","F2.13"],"TURN MODE: The Turn Modes of seeking weapons are as follows:"],"F2.13":["Weapon HET","Each drone and plasma torpedo may make one High Energy Turn per 32 impulses, which lets it turn to face its target when it has overshot.",["F2.12"],"HIGH ENERGY TURNS: All drones and plasma torpedoes may make one High Energy Turn during each period of 32 consecutive impulses on the map. Fighters used as scatter-packs"],"F2.21":["Tracking the target","A seeking weapon must move closer to its target if it can, otherwise stay at the same range. It then tries to keep the target within its forward arc.",["F2.22","F2.12"],"BASIC CONDITIONS: The basic seeking weapon conditions are: 1. If it is possible to move the seeking weapon closer to its target, this must be done. 2. If #1 is impossible, the seeking weapon must, if possible, move in such a manner as to maintain the current distance from the target. All distances are based on the distance at the instant before the seeking weapon moves (C1.313). The seeking weapon cannot be forced to use its HET; see (F2.132). It is possible under some circumstances that a seeking weapon could circle a target only able to hit it by using its HET, but the controlling player is never required to use the HET. E X A M P L E : Stationary target in hex 2215 (having earlier emergency decelerated to launch a wild weasel). Drone ship in hex 2214. …"],"F2.22":["Tracking arc","Having satisfied the rule above, a seeking weapon turns to keep the target in its front (FA) arc, or if that is impossible its wider forward arc.",["F2.21"],"TRACKING ARC: Having satisfied the above conditions, the seeking weapon must move in such a way to place or keep the target in the weapon’s FA firing arc. If this is not possible, the seeking weapon must move in such a way as to place or keep the target in the weapon’s FX firing arc. If this is not possible, this condition is ignored. Note that ship-guided weapons are unaffected by an out-ofarc target, while certain self-guided weapons must have the target in their FA arc or they become inert (FD1.7) and cease to function."],"F2.31":["Impact","A seeking weapon hits when it enters its target's hex or the target enters its hex. The warhead is then reduced by the target's ECM (drones) or cloak, and the damage goes to the shield facing the weapon.",["D6.36","F2.12"],"GENERAL PROCEDURE: Seeking weapons impact upon entering the hex of their target, or upon their target entering the hex occupied by the seeking weapon. See (F2.32) if the seeking weapon was launched in the same hex as its target."],"F3.21":["Control limits","A ship can control as many seeking weapons at once as its sensor rating (usually six). Extra ones are released.",["F3.31","F3.32"],"SHIPS (with certain exceptions below) can control a number of seeking weapons (drones, plasma torpedoes, pseudo-plasma torpedoes, scatter-packs, suicide shuttles) equal to their sensor rating at any given time (usually six). This procedure operates as defined in (F3.3). All seeking weapons are treated equally; a Klingon D7 battlecruiser could simultaneously control two of its own drones, three plasma torpedoes fired by allied Romulan ships, and a suicide shuttle launched by an allied Lyran ship. See (G14.111)"],"F3.31":["Control conditions","To control a seeking weapon the ship needs active fire control, lock-on to the target, and the target and the weapon must be within 35 hexes.",["F3.21","F3.32","D6.6"],"CONDITIONS: To control seeking weapons, a unit must meet all of the following qualifications: 1. The unit must have active fire control operating. 2. The unit must have a lock-on to the target. 3. The unit must be within 35 hexes of the target. 4. The unit must be within 35 hexes of the weapon. 5. The weapon must be within 35 hexes of the target. 6. The unit must have an adequate number of control channels (F3.2). All of the ranges are effective range (D1.4)."],"F3.32":["Loss of control","If any control condition fails the seeking weapon is released. A released drone goes inert and is removed; a plasma torpedo guides itself.",["F3.31","F3.41","F3.42"],"LOSS OF QUALIFICATIONS: If at any point the controlling unit fails to meet or maintain the conditions in (F3.31), the seeking weapons it controls are released (F3.4) and may become inert"],"F3.41":["Release","When control is released another friendly ship can take over; if none does, the drone becomes inert and is removed from play.",["F3.32","F3.42"],"EFFECT OF RELEASE: When control is released, another friendly unit can assume control of that seeking weapon (F3.5). If no SEEKING WEAPONS — F other unit assumes control, the seeking weapon will become inert"],"F3.42":["Self-guiding weapons","Plasma torpedoes (and some special drones) keep their own lock-on and do not need a controlling ship once launched.",["FP4.2","F3.32"],"SELF-GUIDING WEAPONS: Plasma torpedoes (FP4.0), drones with active terminal guidance (FD5.2), and type-VI drones"],"FD1.21":["Launching drones","A launched drone starts on the launching ship, in any direction, but its target must be in its forward arc. The target is named at launch.",["FD1.22","FD4.2"],"PROCEDURE: When launched, the drone is placed on top of the launching ship, facing any direction at the option of the owning player. Drones must have their target in their FA arc when launched. The target ship for each drone must be announced on launch; exception (F3.6)."],"FD1.22":["Drone launch energy","Launching a drone costs no energy but needs fire control to be active.",["FD1.21","D6.6"],"ENERGY: It requires no energy to launch a drone, although fire control must be active. Exception (D19.22) launch under passive fire control."],"FD1.5":["Shooting at drones","Any weapon can fire at a drone; photons, disruptors, plasma bolts and the like suffer four points of natural ECM against it. Only damage equal to the drone's destruction rating (usually 4) destroys it.",["FD1.54"],"FIRING AT DRONES Any type of weapon can be fired at drones, but some (because of their nature) are penalized when doing so."],"FD1.54":["Drone destruction","A drone is destroyed by damage points equal to its rating (4 for the standard types). There are no partial results, and the owner is not told how much damage was done.",["FD1.5"],"DESTRUCTION: A drone is destroyed if it receives damage points equal to its destruction rating (FD2.1). There are no “crippled drones.” The number of damage points required to destroy a drone may be modified by armor (FD12.0). A player is not required to announce the number of damage points that were actually required to destroy a given drone, only that the drone was destroyed by the damage scored against it. EXAMPLE: A type-VI drone is destroyed by a bolted plasma-F at five hexes range. The bolting player only knows that the drone was destroyed, not that it took only three points of damage to do so. If the drone were a type-IV frame with three armor modules (twelve damage points to destroy, six point warhead), the bolting player would be told only that the drone was not destroyed by the bolt."],"FD1.7":["Drones that lose control","A drone whose controller loses control and cannot be handed to another ship becomes inert and is removed.",["F3.41","F3.32"],"if control cannot be transferred (F3.5) or assumed by the weapon itself (F3.42)."],"FD3.1":["Drone racks","A standard type-A rack holds four drones and can launch one per turn. Racks are destroyed by \"DRONE\" hits on the damage chart.",["FD4.2","D4.323"],"TYPE-A The standard “A-rack” or “type-A drone rack” has a capacity of four spaces of drones and can fire one per turn. Unless stated otherwise in the ship descriptions, all drone racks are of this type. Virtually all type-A drone racks were replaced with types B or C in Y175."],"FD4.2":["Launch rate","Each rack launches one drone per turn. In this game a rack must also wait eight impulses between launches.",["FD3.1","F1.22"],"BASIC RATE Unless stated otherwise in the ship descriptions, or when using player-modifications, all ships are presumed to be able to launch one drone from each of their racks each turn."],"FP1.22":["Plasma arming energy","Arming takes three turns with energy in exact amounts (see the arming table). A missed turn loses the arming so far and the launcher starts again.",["FP1.24","FP2.51"],"ENERGY: The energy to arm the torpedo must be applied over a three-turn period in EXACTLY the increments shown in (FP2.51). No more energy can be allocated; if any less energy than required is allocated, the weapon is lost and must begin arming all over again. A plasma torpedo launcher can begin arming in mid turn through the application of reserve power (H7.2), but not on the same turn in which that a plasma torpedo was launched by that launcher."],"FP1.24":["Holding plasma","A fully armed plasma torpedo not fired on the third turn can be held for a small energy cost (plasma-S two, plasma-G one, plasma-F none, or one if downloaded; a plasma-R on a ship cannot be held, FP2.12). If the cost is not paid it is ejected. There is no eight-impulse holding period.",["FP2.51","FP2.12"],"HOLDING: If not fired on the third turn, the torpedo can be held for the appropriate energy cost (FP2.5). If the holding energy is not paid, the torpedo is ejected. There is no eight-impulse holding period as per (FP1.71). Mark an “H” on the EAF to avoid confusion."],"FP1.31":["Plasma launch","A plasma torpedo can be launched during any impulse of the turn on which it finishes arming (or any later turn if held).",["FP1.312","FP1.32"],"PROCEDURE: A plasma torpedo may be launched during the Impulse Activity Segment of any impulse during the turn in which arming is completed."],"FP1.312":["Launch arcs","A plasma torpedo must be launched in a direction its tube can fire and with the target in its forward arc. In this game the target must be in the tube's listed arcs.",["FP1.31"],"The torpedo must be launched in accordance with the tracking arcs (FP3.0) available for that tube. When the torpedo is placed on the board, it must be facing in a direction available to that launch tube and must have the target in its (the torpedo’s) FA arc. FP— PLASMA TORPEDOES STAR FLEET BATTLES The target of a plasma torpedo must be announced when it is placed on the board, unless using (F3.6)."],"FP1.32":["Warhead strength","The warhead strength depends on the type (R, S, G, F) and on how many hexes the torpedo has travelled; closer targets get a bigger hit. It is looked up on the Plasma Torpedo Table.",["FP1.51","FP1.611"],"WARHEAD: When a plasma torpedo is placed on the board, the owning player must state the warhead strength if there is an enemy unit within 35 hexes (FP1.323)."],"FP1.4":["Plasma movement","A plasma torpedo moves 32 hexes per turn, steers itself, makes one HET per 32 impulses and lasts 32 impulses.",["F2.11","F2.13"],"MOVEMENT"],"FP1.51":["Plasma strength at impact","The strength is worked out when it hits: first by distance travelled, then reduced by phaser damage. At long range it can fade to nothing before hitting.",["FP1.32","FP1.611"],"STRENGTH CALCULATION: The warhead strength of a plasma torpedo is determined at the instant of impact, based on two factors: the distance that the torpedo has traveled (it grows weaker the farther it travels) and damage done to it (FP1.6) by phasers and (possibly) other effects. This may be further adjusted by the effects of electronic warfare (D6.36). The warhead strength vs range (for each type) is shown on the Plasma Torpedo Table (FP1.53). The warhead strength decreases with range, as is shown on the plasma torpedo tables. Once the warhead strength reaches zero, the torpedo has no further effect or function and the counter is removed from the board. Terrain-induced movement (black hole, nebula) does not count as movement for purposes of lowering the warhead strength."],"FP1.61":["Shooting plasma torpedoes","Only phasers (or impact with a large object such as a planet or asteroid, and some terrain) can damage a plasma torpedo, which is why the AI and the fire card offer phasers against plasma.",["FP1.611"],"DAMAGING PLASMA TORPEDOES: Plasma torpedoes may be fired at only by phasers (of any type). ONLY phasers or impact with a large object (such as a planet or asteroid), and some other terrain types listed below, will damage a plasma torpedo."],"FP1.611":["Phaser damage to plasma","Every two points of phaser damage reduce the warhead by one point (below the table value). Enough damage can destroy the torpedo.",["FP1.61","FP1.51"],"Every two points of damage by phaser fire reduces the warhead strength by one. Note that this reduction is below the normal strength shown on the Plasma Torpedo Table."],"FP2.12":["Plasma-R hold","A plasma-R on a ship cannot be held; if not fired after the third turn it is lost. Only bases can hold a plasma-R.",["FP1.24"],"NON-HOLDABLE: In the case of ships carrying plasma-R, the weapon cannot be held if it is not fired before the end of the third turn of arming. A starbase (but not a smaller base) armed with a plasma-R could hold it when fully charged. FP— PLASMA TORPEDOES STAR FLEET BATTLES"],"FP2.51":["Plasma arming costs","Plasma-R 2+2+5, plasma-S 2+2+4, plasma-G 2+2+3, plasma-F 1+1+3 over the three arming turns. Enveloping or shotgun versions cost R 10, S 8, G 6 (not F). Holding costs: R 4 (only a starbase can hold a plasma-R, FP2.12), S 2, G 1, F 0 (1 if it was downloaded from a larger launcher).",["FP1.22","FP1.24"],"ARMING COST: The cost of arming a plasma torpedo is as follows: Type Turn #1 Turn #2 Turn #3 EPT/SG Hold Cost R S G F NA 0*"],"FP4.1":["Lock-on at launch","A launching ship must have a lock-on to the target when it launches a plasma torpedo.",["FP4.2"],"LOCK-ON AT TIME OF LAUNCH"],"FP4.2":["Self-guidance","After launch, a plasma torpedo guides itself, with its own lock-on, and can track targets in any direction within 35 hexes.",["FP4.23","F3.42"],"SELF-GUIDING ABILITY"],"FP4.23":["Plasma guidance limits","Plasma torpedoes keep their own lock within 35 hexes, but can still be fooled by EW, cloaks and other effects. A target beyond 35 hexes is lost.",["FP4.2","G13.334"],"CONDITIONS: Plasma torpedoes have superior guidance systems to ATG drones. They can maintain their own lock-on within 35 hexes and can track targets in any arc. Note, however, that plasma torpedoes are still distracted or otherwise affected by wild weasels, EW, cloaks, etc. Plasma torpedoes are not affected by some scout functions that affect drones; see (G24.225) and (G24.233). Plasma torpedoes (except type-D) are not affected by chaff (D11.6). Ballistically launched (F4.0) plasma torpedoes cannot acquire their own targets, but might be launched at ground targets (P2.713). Plasma torpedoes cannot use tame boar targeting (FD5.252) as type-III drones can."],"FP4.5":["Plasma and cloaks","A plasma torpedo not controlled by another unit rolls for its own lock-on to a cloaked target under G13.334. If it is controlled by a ship, the ship rolls first; if the ship fails, the torpedo is released and may roll for itself.",["G13.334","FP4.2","FP4.31"],"CLOAKS"],"G13.11":["Switching it on or off","The cloak can be turned on or off during the cloak step of any impulse. On starts the fade-out (G13.14), off starts the fade-in (G13.15). The fade can carry over into the next turn.",["G13.113","G13.14","G13.15"],"OPERATION: The cloaking device can be turned on or off during the Activate/Deactivate Cloaking Device Step of the Cloaking Device Stage of any impulse."],"G13.113":["Once on, once off","A cloak can be turned on only once and off only once per turn. Paying twice does not get around this.",["G13.11","G13.232"],"A cloaking device can only be turned on one time during each turn and can only be turned off one time during each turn. No unit can have more than one cloaking device."],"G13.116":["Not paying for the cloak","If you do not allocate energy for a cloak that was on, it counts as turning it off: fade-in begins on impulse 1 and the ship is not instantly visible. If you do pay on the next turn, the ship stays cloaked.",["G13.117","G13.23"],"If a cloaked ship does not allocate energy for cloaking, it begins fade in during Impulse #1 of that turn. It does NOT become fully uncloaked immediately. Not allocating the energy to continue operating a cloaking device during Energy Allocation counts as turning the device off for that turn (G13.11); fade in begins on Impulse #1."],"G13.14":["Fade-out","The cloak is fully active five impulses after it is switched on. During those impulses the effective range to the ship increases by one hex per impulse (+1, +2, +3, +4, +5), phasing in the +5 range penalty. The ship is under the cloak restrictions (no fire control) from the moment it is switched on. The range doubling for lost lock-on only begins once the cloak is fully active and lock-on has been lost.",["G13.302","G13.331","G13.36"],"ACTIVATING THE CLOAKING DEVICE: If the device is not operating, the owning player may announce that it has been turned on during the Cloaking Device Stage of the Sequence of Play. However, the device does not become fully active [i.e., gain all of the benefits of (G13.30)] until five impulses after the announcement. G — SYSTEMS STAR FLEET BATTLES Further, from the time that the announcement is made, the ship is under the restrictions of (G13.5). EXAMPLE: The ship announces on Impulse #20 that it is activating its cloaking device. The ship is under many of the restrictions of the device immediately, but does not gain all of the benefits until the Cloaking Device Stage of Impulse #25. During the five-impulse period when the device is being turned on, the ship “fades” out of the tracking scopes of other ships. …"],"G13.15":["Fade-in","When the cloak is switched off the range penalty phases out over five impulses (+5, +4, +3, +2, +1, then 0), and the +x2 doubling is gone at once. The ship cannot fire or launch until fire control is active and fade-in is complete.",["G13.14","G13.36","G13.51"],"DEACTIVATING THE CLOAKING DEVICE: If the device is operating, the owning player may announce that it has been turned off during the Cloak Stage of the Impulse Activity Segment of any impulse. At that point, the device is off and the ship may be locked-on to by enemy ships, losing the (G13.301) benefit. However, the ship itself may not lock-on to any enemy ship or fire/launch any weapons until its fire control becomes active AND fade-in is complete. Alternatively, it could use passive fire control (D19.0) AFTER fade-in is complete. EXAMPLE: The ship dropped its cloak on Impulse #10; it could not fire/launch weapons until Impulse #15. During the five-impulse period when the device is being turned off, the ship “fades” onto the tracking scopes of other ships. …"],"G13.23":["Paying once per turn","The cost is paid once per turn however long the cloak is used, and whether it is turned on, off or both. Unused energy is not refunded. It does not give ECM or any other effect.",["G13.21","G13.116","G13.232"],"ENERGY COST: The cost of operating the cloaking device is paid once per turn, regardless of whether the device is operating one impulse, 32 impulses, or some number in between, and regardless of whether or not the device was activated, deactivated, or both (in either order) during that turn. If the device is not active during part (or all) of the turn, the ship does not recover any of the unused energy."],"G13.301":["Penalty 1: no lock-on","Enemy units cannot lock on to a fully cloaked ship, so the range to it is doubled (D6.123). This does not apply during fade-in or fade-out, or if the enemy has kept a lock-on (G13.33).",["G13.31","G13.33","G13.333","D6.123"],"NO LOCK-ON: The most basic effect of the cloaking device is that enemy units cannot lock onto the cloaked ship. This imposes a number of restrictions, e.g., (D6.12) and (D6.13), and causes the range to the cloaked ship to be doubled (D6.123). This penalty does not apply during the fade period (G13.14) or (G13.15). A lock-on can be gained to a cloaked ship by various means, as seen in (G13.333), (G7.99), and (G13.4). If a lock-on is achieved (and maintained), this penalty does not apply."],"G13.311":["Seeking weapons without lock-on","Seeking weapons cannot work without a lock-on to the cloaked ship and are removed from play. Plasma torpedoes and other self-guiding weapons use G13.334 instead.",["G13.31","G13.334"],"Seeking weapons cannot function without a lock-on"],"G13.331":["Lock-on retention roll","Roll one die when the fade-out is finished. The lock-on is kept on a roll of P or less, where P = S - EW - RF + SF - 4. S is the hunter's sensor rating, EW is the net electronic warfare (cloaked ship's ECM minus the hunter's ECCM, converted with the D6.34 chart; if negative it is subtracted as a negative number), RF is the range factor (true range 1-4: 0; 5-10: 1; 11-15: 2; 16-20: 3; 21-30: 4; 31-40: 5; 41 or more: 6) and SF the speed factor from the cloaked ship's maneuver rate (1-4: 0; 5-8: 1; 9-12: 2; 13-15: 3; 16-17: 4; 18: 5; 19 or more: 6). The game uses the maneuver rate (C2.42): speed, plus 5 in an impulse in which the cloaked ship makes an HET.",["G13.332","G13.333","C2.42"],""],"G13.333":["Reacquiring lock-on","A ship that has lost lock-on can try again whenever conditions improve. The chance is P = S - EW - RF + SF - 10, so it is very unlikely without help such as scouts.",["G13.331","G13.301"],"or (G13.4) unless it uncloaks; see (G13.63)."],"G13.334":["Self-guiding seeking weapons","Plasma torpedoes (and ATG drones) controlled by a ship have a lock-on while that ship does. If it fails, they are released and may roll once for their own lock-on, with sensor rating 6 and no outside ECCM (plasma has 3 built-in). Uncontrolled ones roll for themselves. Failure removes them from play.",["FP4.5","G13.311","FP4.31"],"for the retention rule; self-guiding seeking weapons have a sensor rating of six."],"G13.35":["Seeking weapons arriving at a cloaked ship","A seeking weapon that reaches the cloaked ship may fail to find it. The fire adjustment chart G13.37 decides how much of the warhead is left (full, half, quarter or nothing).",["G13.37","G13.303"],"SEEKING WEAPONS: If a seeking weapon enters the hex occupied by the cloaked target ship, there is a substantial probability that it will not be able to find the target. Use (G13.37) to determine the damage caused."],"G13.37":["Fire adjustment chart","Roll one die for each weapon: 1 or 2 full damage, 3 or 4 half, 5 or 6 a quarter, 7 or more none. Round halves up. This replaces EW shifts (the cloak is the maximum effect). Applied to each weapon individually, not to the sum.",["G13.303","G13.361","G13.371"],"FIRE ADJUSTMENT CHART Roll one die to determine the actual result of each individual weapon. Note that in the case of a narrow salvo (E1.6), a single die roll is used for the entire volley. Direct fire weapons are affected by (G13.34); seeking weapons by (G13.35). Type-VI drones ARE affected. For additional data, see (G13.303) for penalty conditions,"],"G13.51":["Basic restriction","A cloaked ship has fire control inactive. This applies while the cloak is operating and through every fade period, even if fire control has been switched on during fade-in.",["G13.511","G13.512","G13.513","G13.13"],"BASIC RESTRICTION: A cloaked ship has its fire control system in the inactive mode (D6.64) and cannot use passive fire control (D19.0). These restrictions apply while the cloaking device is operating and during all fade periods, even if fire control has been activated during a fade-in period. Note that the reference here to the fire control being considered inactive does not mean that a weasel would not be voided if the fire control was activated as the ship came out of cloak."],"G13.512":["No launching or guiding","A cloaked ship cannot launch seeking weapons, even self-guiding ones or those on a ballistic course, and cannot guide ones already launched.",["G13.51"],"A cloaked ship cannot launch seeking weapons (even those with ATG or on a ballistic course) or guide those already launched."],"H5.0":["Batteries","Batteries store energy from the warp engines and give it back later.",["H5.3","H5.4"],"and (H7.0). 17. TRACTOR AND NEGATIVE TRACTOR BEAM: Energy allocated here is used to tractor various objects or to prevent the ship from being tractored. See (G7.0) for details. 18. TRANSPORTERS: Power allocated here is used to operate the ship’s transporters. See (G8.0) for details. 1 9 . Several lines are provided to allocate energy to miscellaneous systems not specifically listed above. Examples of these would include electronic warfare, cloaking devices, or shuttles. 20. TOTAL POWER USED: This line is used as a final mathematical check to make sure that you have allocated the correct amount of power. It also serves to determine if you have discharged any batteries. 21. BATTERY POWER USED: This is a record of the number of batteries that were discharged on the current turn. It is used to adjust lines 5 and 6 on the next turn. …"],"H5.3":["Battery capacity","Batteries store a fixed amount of power. They are recharged in Energy Allocation and discharged for energy allocation or as reserve power.",["H5.4","H7.41"],"CAPACITY AND USE The power stored in batteries may be used when extra energy is needed; the batteries can be recharged during Energy Allocation."],"H5.4":["Using batteries","A battery can only be discharged for energy allocation or for reserve power. Battery power cannot be used for movement, photon arming or overloads, which need warp energy.",["H5.3","H7.41"],"LEGAL USE A battery cannot be discharged unless the energy is being used for something in the Energy Allocation Procedure (B3.0) or in midturn as Reserve Power under the provisions of (H7.0). Note that, if a player wishes to discharge batteries, he can use some or all of his battery power instead of using the full amount of output from his power-producing systems. Power from batteries is neither warp nor impulse power and, as such, cannot be used for functions which require those specific types of power. Exceptions: Reserve Warp (H7.4), Reserve Impulse (H7.47), Second-Generation X-batteries (XH5.1)."],"H6.0":["Phaser capacitor","All ships have a capacitor that stores energy for phasers; it must be charged during energy allocation. Each phaser shot drains it, and a phaser-3 shot costs half a point.",["H7.0","B3.21"],"PHASER CAPACITORS All ships have a “phaser capacitor.” This is a special storage battery (actually a network of smaller capacitors) used to fire the ship’s phasers. The terms “capacitor” and “capacitors” are both technically correct and may be used interchangeably when talking about the phaser capacitor on a ship. Capacitors must be energized (E2.3) before they can hold power. Some other systems [example ESGs (G23.24)] also have capacitors; these are not linked to or part of the phaser capacitor."],"H7.0":["Reserve power","Energy from batteries can be used during the turn (reserve power) to charge the phaser capacitor, reinforce shields, boost EW or overload weapons.",["H7.2","H7.41"],"RESERVE POWER Energy can be added to the phaser capacitor as allocated power during the Energy Allocation Phase (B3.0) or by using Reserve Power (H7.0) during the Impulse Procedure and Record-Keeping Phases. Ships may use their batteries as a source of reserve power. Note specifically that unallocated power from engines or reactors is NOT treated as reserve power; it was simply never produced (B3.4). Excess movement energy released by Emergency Deceleration"],"H7.134":["Reserve power when damaged","Reserve power can be spent after damage is scored but before it is allocated inside the ship, usually as general reinforcement for shield damage that was just done.",["H7.2","D3.341"],"One of the most critical moments for the user of reserve power is at the time that damage is scored on the ship. After damage is scored (i.e., after die rolls, the movement of seeking weapons, asteroid, etc.) and the number of damage points is known, there are relatively few things that reserve power can be used for (before the internal damage is actually allocated, with the chance that batteries might be destroyed). These include the following options only: • Reinforcing shields and providing general reinforcement, but not raising them or increasing their level. Decisions on whether H — POWER STAR FLEET BATTLES or not to apply reinforcement energy are made in response to each given step of the Direct-Fire Weapons Fire Stage (6D2). A player must decide about reserve reinforcement before seeing whether “later” weapons hit. • Increasing the level of PA panels. …"],"H7.2":["Uses of reserve power","Reserve power can boost ECM or ECCM, raise shields, add general reinforcement, charge phasers, or overload weapons during the turn.",["H7.54","D6.312"],"SOME USES OF RESERVE POWER Reserve power can (within the limits of the rules) be used to: SYSTEMS Increase the amount of ECM or ECCM (D6.315) being generated or lent by (G24.2114) or (J4.93). Generate reserve EW for use under (G24.211). Raise shields or increase their level (H7.34). Reinforce shields (H7.34), even after damage is s cored (H7.134) or a boarding attempt is announced (H7.341). Activate or reinforce PA panels (D10.24); see (H7.346). Activate a cloaking device (G13.21). Operate mine detecting systems (M7.21). Activate active fire control (D6.6). Provide power for tractors (G7.411) or transporters (G8.13). Begin arming a wild weasel (J3.12). Increase speed through (C12.24), use warp Tactical Maneuvers (C5.2), begin Erratic Maneuvers (C10.11), or make an High Energy Turn (C6.2); these are movement-related functions which can be performed with reserve warp engine power"],"H7.38":["Destroyed batteries","If a battery is destroyed its stored reserve power is lost immediately.",["H7.0"],"DESTROYED BATTERIES: If the battery is destroyed before the power is used, the reserve power (warp or non-warp) is lost immediately and cannot be used later. Because of this, batteries are key targets for hit-and-run raids (D7.8). NOTE: This power could be allocated under (H7.134), but that could only happen before damage was allocated."],"H7.41":["Recharging batteries","Warp power allocated to recharge batteries becomes reserve warp power. Other unallocated warp power is lost; it cannot be used as reserve.",["H7.43","H5.4"],"BATTERIES: Some warp power can be allocated to recharging batteries; see (H7.43). This cannot be more than the capacity of the discharged batteries. This specific warp power, and no other warp power, is treated as reserve warp power. Otherwise unallocated warp power cannot be used as reserve power. Note: Warp power from AWRs is restricted by (H7.45)."],"H7.43":["The energy cycle","Batteries can be discharged and recharged in the same turn. The usual cycle: use battery power during allocation, then put warp power into recharging them for use next turn.",["H7.41"],"ENERGY CYCLE: Batteries can be discharged and energy allocated to recharge them at the same time. The most common procedure is to use whatever power is in the batteries for various functions (which cannot include recharging batteries) during Energy Allocation, and then to allocate warp engine power to recharge the batteries. That warp power is then treated as Reserve Warp Engine Power (H7.41), which is the most flexible type of reserve power. This is a very common practice, and it is regarded as a “standard operating procedure” when there is sufficient unallocated warp power available. Players are not required to use this procedure, but are advised that doing so will provide the maximum amount of reserve warp engine power, which is the most flexible type. …"],"H7.54":["Overloads from reserve power","Reserve power can overload a weapon that has an overload function, such as a photon (reserve warp only) or disruptor.",["E4.45","E3.55"],"OVERLOADS: Reserve power can be used to provide overload energy to a weapon, whether about to fire or not (assuming the weapon has an overload function). This includes additional overload energy to a weapon, such as a photon torpedo, with variable overload levels. See (E11.63) for specifics in the case of PPDs. The application of reserve warp power to overload a photon torpedo would not increase the holding cost unless the torpedo is held until the end of the turn, when the cost would be increased for the next turn; see (E4.412)."],"S4.1":["Phaser weapon status","Scenarios set a Weapon Status; higher numbers mean phasers are already charged at the start.",["H6.0"],"which keeps the phasers energized. In wartime, or when the ship has been summoned to the point of battle, scenarios will often specify a Weapon Status of one or higher. E — DIRECT-FIRE WEAPONS"],"SG1.0":["The Duel","The introductory scenario: two hostile cruisers meet in deep space. Set-up (SG1.2): your ship in 4203 heading E and the opponent in 0730 heading A, both at speed 15 and Weapon Status II. It ends when all forces on one side are destroyed, captured or have disengaged (SG1.3). The book's map rule (SG1.41) is a floating map where you may disengage only through the 42xx or xx01 edges and the opponent through 01xx or xx30; leaving by any other edge counts as destroyed. This game keeps a fixed 42 by 30 map unless you tick the floating-map option. Scored by the Standard Victory Conditions (S2.2). Captain's Basic Set page 207, Master Rulebook page 400.",["S2.2","S4.1","B2.3"],"THE DUEL. The BPV of the CC is 137; the D7 is 121, so the Klingon player scores sixteen points. The Federation player also purchases some additional weapons and equipment (nineteen points worth), so the Klingon player scores an additional nineteen points. In the course of the scenario, both players scored internal damage on the enemy. The Klingon ship was crippled (S2.4), but the Federation ship disengaged to avoid being hit by several Klingon drones. Thus, the Federation player scored 61 points for crippling the D7 (121 x 50% rounded up); the Klingon player scored 34 points for forcing the Federation ship to disengage (137 x 25% = 34.25 rounded down), plus the 35 points he received for play balance. The score is thus 69 to 61 in favor of the Klingon ship, which thereby won the scenario. Since this score (69/61) is 113%, the Klingon won a marginal victory (S2.3). …"],"G13.0":["The cloaking device","Used by most Romulan ships. A cloaked ship stays on the map but cannot fire or launch while cloaked, and enemies find it very hard to lock on to and hit. If it is spotted while uncloaking it can be badly hurt.",["G13.1","G13.2","G13.3","G13.5"],"THE CLOAKING DEVICE Most Romulan ships (and some Orions) are equipped with this device, which makes detection of the ship almost impossible. However, while the ship itself cannot be seen, the effect of its magnetic field on light from the background of stars can be seen and will give at least a general idea of where the ship is. This invisibility comes at a price: the ship cannot fire weapons while cloaked. Even worse, if the ship is accurately spotted, it could be badly damaged by enemy weapons while trying to uncloak. Romulan ships use the cloaking device to make secret approaches to enemy forces, to escape after an attack, and to protect themselves while reloading their plasma torpedoes during a battle."],"G13.1":["Operating the cloak","The device is turned on or off in the cloak step of any impulse, once each per turn. Turning it on starts a five-impulse fade-out; turning it off starts a five-impulse fade-in.",["G13.11","G13.113","G13.14","G13.15"],"OPERATION OF CLOAKING DEVICE The cloaking device is operated by these rules."],"G13.115":["Reversing a fade","A ship can stop a fade and reverse it: it fades back the same number of impulses it had already faded. The whole thing counts as the one fade allowed that turn, and a reversed fade cannot be reversed again. In the game, click the fading button.",["G13.14","G13.15"],"The player can stop/reverse the fade-in or fade-out process during any Cloaking Device Stage, but the ship must then fade back out (or in) the same number of impulses that it had has already faded in (or out). The combined action counts as the only fade-in and fade-out allowed during that turn. The ship cannot stop at a point partially faded-in/out and await developments, but must either complete the process or reverse it. Once reversed, that specific fade cannot be reversed again. If a fade-in is reversed, the ship does not pay for a second activation of the cloaking device. SYSTEMS — G EXAMPLE: A Warbird turns its cloaking device off on Impulse #10 (+5), fading in during Impulses #11 (+4) and #12 (+3). …"],"G13.117":["Staying cloaked between turns","A ship cloaked on one turn that pays the cost next turn remains cloaked; it is not exposed while energy is reallocated.",["G13.116"],"If the ship was cloaked on one turn and pays the energy during Energy Allocation to continue the operation of the device on the next turn, the ship remains cloaked. It is not exposed at the end of each turn while the captain reallocates the ship’s energy."],"G13.13":["Fire control while cloaked","While the cloak is on or fading the ship cannot have fire control active. When the cloak is turned off, fire control can be reactivated (four impulses), but the ship cannot fire until fade-in is complete as well.",["G13.51","G13.133"],"FIRE CONTROL: While the device is active, the ship cannot have its fire control systems in the active mode. See (G13.51)."],"G13.2":["Cloak energy","Each ship's cloak cost is printed on its SSD (Romulan War Eagle 6, KR 20, K5R 6). The Basic Set chart is superseded by the SSD.",["G13.21","G13.22","G13.23"],"ENERGY COST OF OPERATION The ships that have the cloaking device and the cost for using the device are shown in (G13.21). NOTE: The printed power costs on published SSDs supercede any cost listed in this rule section and are specific to the individual ship. Generally ships with more power generating systems will have a higher cloak cost, and ships with fewer power generating systems may have a reduced cloak cost than that listed for their size class in this rule section."],"G13.21":["Cloak cost","The cost is paid during energy allocation (or with reserve power). If the full cost is not paid the device cannot be activated. Costs: Warbird 1, War Eagle 6, K5R 6, KR 20, Orion CR 10, base station 8, battle station 12, starbase 40.",["G13.2","G13.23"],"ENERGY COST OF OPERATION: Energy must be paid to activate a cloaking device. This is usually paid during Energy Allocation, but may be paid with reserve power (H7.2) or contingent reserve (H7.6). If the full energy cost is not provided, the device cannot be activated. The cost per turn is as follows: Base Station....................8 Romulan K5R ..........................6 Battlestation ..................12 Romulan KR...........................20 Starbase ........................40 Romulan War Eagle ...............6 Orion CR........................10 Romulan Warbird....................1 The above chart includes all ships in Basic Set. All ships have their cloak energy cost on their SSD. Also see Annex #7H in Advanced Missions for an expanded list of units."],"G13.22":["Warp shutdown","A ship that shuts down its warp engines (allocates no warp energy) can cloak for less: size class 1 costs 30, 2 costs 6, 3 costs 4, 4 costs 2 (the game uses each ship's own printed figure). A ship with its warp engines shut down cannot move under warp power. Tick 'warp shut down' beside the cloak in Energy Allocation.",["G13.21"],"WARP SHUTDOWN: If a ship shuts down (does not use for power) or has dropped (G12.6) its warp engines, it can cloak for a lower cost based on its size class. Size classes and reduced costs are listed below. Size Class 1..................30 Size Class 3.............................4 Size Class 2....................6 Size Class 4.............................2 This “shut down” is accomplished during Energy Allocation by simply not allocating any energy from the engines. It is not the same thing as"],"G13.232":["No double payment","Paying the cost twice does not allow a second activation in a turn.",["G13.113"],"A ship cannot pay the cost twice (or more) in order to avoid the restrictions of (G13.113). STAR FLEET BATTLES"],"G13.3":["Combat against cloaked ships","Cloaked ships are harder to hit. There are three penalties: no lock-on (range doubled), +5 range, and a damage reduction chart. Some can be lost if lock-on is kept.",["G13.30","G13.31","G13.32","G13.37"],"COMBAT AGAINST CLOAKED SHIPS Ships operating cloaking devices are more difficult to hit with weapons. Specific penalties are imposed."],"G13.30":["The three penalties","Firing at a cloaked ship: no lock-on (G13.301), +5 range (G13.302), and the fire adjustment chart (G13.303). Under certain circumstances some of these do not apply.",["G13.301","G13.302","G13.303"],"PENALTIES: When firing at a cloaked ship, there are three basic penalties, as described herein. Under certain circumstances, some of these penalties may not apply."],"G13.302":["Penalty 2: +5 range","Five is added to the effective range after any doubling. It is phased in or out during the fade. Example: a ship at true range 3 with no lock-on fires at 2 x 3 + 5 = 11; with lock-on retained, at 3 + 5 = 8.",["G13.14","G13.15","G13.301"],"RANGE PENALTY: When firing at a cloaked ship, five is added to the range when calculating the effective range. This is done after the effects of (G13.301) are accounted for. During the fade period (G13.14) and (G13.15), this penalty is phased in (or out) gradually. This penalty is ignored if the firing unit has a tractor link to the cloaked ship (G7.993). The effective range (after both penalties) is used for tactical intelligence (D17.221)."],"G13.303":["Penalty 3: damage adjustment","All weapons (direct fire and seeking) fired at a cloaked ship are adjusted by the fire adjustment chart G13.37. This replaces the EW shift; the cloak is the maximum EW effect.",["G13.37","D6.34"],"DAMAGE ADJUSTMENT: All weapons (direct or seeking) fired at a cloaked ship are adjusted (probably downward) by (G13.37). Cloaking is a type of EW effect, but uses a different system. EW penalties for firing at a cloaked ship (D6.35) and (D6.36) are ignored; the chart in (G13.37) effectively IS the EW shift. This penalty does not apply to non-weapons (tractors, SFGs, transporters, etc.) There is no EW die roll as per (D6.37). Type-VI dogfight drones ARE affected. Exception: (G13.344)."],"G13.31":["Combat without a lock-on","All three penalties apply. Seeking weapons cannot function without a lock-on and are removed from play (self-guiding ones follow G13.334). Direct fire is doubled in range and +5; at true range 0 the effective range is 5.",["G13.311","G13.312","G13.334"],"COMBAT WITHOUT A LOCK-ON: If the enemy unit has no lock-on, all of the penalties in (G13.30) apply. Specific cases include:"],"G13.312":["Direct fire without lock-on","Direct-fire weapons suffer the penalties of G13.30: double the true range, then add five. There is no exception for range 0.",["G13.31","G13.34"],"Direct-fire weapons are penalized because of the poor firecontrol solution available. This is reflected by the penalties in (G13.30). See (G13.34) for additional conditions. There is no exclusion for Range Zero. You double the actual range (for the loss of lock-on), and add five. Effective range is five. Note that this is the same way you’d perform the “doubling” if you failed your lock-on roll for any other reason."],"G13.32":["Combat with a lock-on","If the enemy keeps its lock-on, the doubling does not apply, but the +5 range and the damage chart do. Seeking weapons can be launched and guided while the lock-on is kept.",["G13.33","G13.302","G13.303"],"COMBAT WITH A LOCK-ON: If the enemy unit maintains a lock-on to a cloaked ship, penalty (G13.301) does not apply. Penalty"],"G13.33":["Retaining a lock-on","If a unit had a lock-on before the cloak went on, it can try to keep it by die roll when the fade-out ends. If it fails, it has no lock-on and the range doubles.",["G13.331","G13.332","G13.333"],"RETAINING A LOCK-ON: If a firing unit had a lock-on to the cloaked ship before the cloaking device was activated, or if the firing unit is able to achieve a lock-on while the cloaking device is operating through (G13.333) or (G13.4) or other causes, there is a possibility that the lock-on can be retained. Note that, if a ship enters a scenario cloaked, it cannot be locked onto except through"],"G13.332":["New rolls","A new retention roll is made whenever the equation changes in favour of the cloaked ship (for example more ECM or a lower speed). Changes in the hunter's favour do not need a new roll.",["G13.331"],"NEW DIE ROLLS: A new die roll to retain the lock-on (i.e., another chance for the hunting unit to lose lock-on) is made whenever conditions (as defined by the equation) have changed in favor of the cloaked ship."],"G13.34":["Direct fire at a cloaked ship","Use the effective range to decide hits and phaser damage, then reduce the damage with G13.37. Disruptors, photons and other non-phaser weapons use the true range to decide damage. If the effective range is beyond the weapon's maximum it cannot fire.",["G13.341","G13.342","G13.343","G13.37"],"DIRECT-FIRE WEAPONS: For all direct-fire weapons, use the effective range (D1.4) to determine the probability of a hit. [This effective range will be adjusted by (G13.30) to account for the circumstances.] In all cases, the adjustment chart (G13.37) is used to adjust the effectiveness of the fire. If the range (as adjusted for the cloak) exceeds the maximum range of the weapon, it should be painfully obvious to just about everyone that the weapon isn’t going to do any damage."],"G13.341":["Which range for damage","Phasers use the effective range for their damage. Disruptors, photons and similar weapons use the true range for damage, but the effective range for the chance to hit.",["G13.34"],"GENERAL: For phasers, fusion beams, and tractorrepulsor beams, use the effective range to determine the damage caused by the weapon. Note that Hydran fighter fusion beams fired at a true range of two hexes would require only one charge even if the damage is rolled for an effective range greater than two hexes. For disruptors, plasma bolts, hellbores, plasmatic pulsar devices, armed probes, and photon torpedoes, use the true range to determine the damage caused by a hit. ADDs cannot be fired at cloaked units [except from inside the shuttle bay (G7.814)]. Maulers use (G13.344). UIMs cannot be used unless the UIM can function at both the true and effective range."],"G13.342":["Minimum range","Weapons with a minimum range (such as non-overloaded photons) cannot fire if the true range is below it, even if the effective range is higher.",["G13.34"],"MINIMUM RANGE: Note that weapons with a specified minimum range (e.g., non-overloaded photon torpedoes, PPDs, nonoverloaded disruptors, etc.) cannot be fired if the true range is less than the stated minimum, even if the effective range is greater than the stated minimum."],"G13.343":["Overloads","Overloaded direct-fire weapons may be fired at a cloaked ship. Feedback damage is not reduced by the chart.",["G13.34"],"OVERLOADS: Overloaded direct-fire weapons may be fired at a cloaked ship. See (D6.126). Feedback damage from overloaded weapons (if any) is not reduced by (G13.37)."],"G13.36":["Fade-period options","During a fade the cloaked player picks, for the whole fade, either (a) the G13.37 chart with the fade range penalty added to the die roll and then 5 subtracted, ignoring EW, or (b) no chart and the normal EW shifts. The game offers both: the selector beside the cloak button is read when a fade starts; the computer always uses (a).",["G13.14","G13.37"],"FADE PERIOD: During the fade period (i.e., the effective range is no longer doubled but some adjustment is added to the range), the player controlling the fading (target) ship can use either of the following procedures at his choice. This choice is made (and announced) when fade begins. Whichever he selects must be used for all firing units during the entire fade period."],"G13.361":["Fade option (a)","Roll on the G13.37 chart, add the penalty hexes to the die roll, then subtract five. Ignore EW. A result below 1 counts as 1.",["G13.36","G13.37"],"Use the chart in (G13.37), but add the penalty hexes to the die roll and then subtract five from the die roll. Ignore any EW effects."],"G13.371":["Chart rounding","A die result below one counts as one. Fractions of 0.5 or more round up, 0.499 or less round down.",["G13.37"],"A result of less than one is considered to be one. [A shift could be caused by (G13.62).] Round fractions of 0.5 and more up, of 0.499 and less down. See (G22.73). A positive modifier can result from the effects of (G13.361) when combined with (G13.62)."],"G13.4":["Losing cloak effects","Several actions \"void\" a cloak, such as hitting a mine, using transporters, being held in a tractor, entering a web, or taking explosion damage. Enemies can then gain a lock-on, though the +5 range and chart still apply. In the game: ship explosions (D5) and asteroid damage void the cloak, a web hex of strength 1 or more exposes the ship, and a ship that holds the cloaked one in a tractor beam loses the +5 range. Mines, ESGs, shuttles and PFs do not occur in a Duel.",["G13.40","G13.401"],"LOSING CLOAKING DEVICE EFFECTS There are several ways in which a cloaked ship could lose some of the benefits of being cloaked. Many are listed in this section. This is known as “voiding” a cloaking device; the effect is to allow a lockon and cancel the (G13.301) penalty. There are other means of voiding a cloaking device than those listed here. See ESGs (G23.62) and (G13.57), and also mines (G13.55). Having a cloak voided is the worst thing that can happen as the cloaked ship loses the (G13.301) benefit and retains the penalties. If the ship voids its cloaking device by any of the procedures noted, it is still under the restrictions of (G13.4), (G13.5), and other relevant rules until the device is deactivated and fade-in is complete. Specifically, it cannot fire weapons until the device is deactivated and fade-in is complete. …"],"G13.40":["Voided cloak","A voided cloak lets others gain a lock-on, cancelling the doubled range but not the +5 and the chart.",["G13.4","G13.401"],"EFFECT OF VOIDED CLOAK: The primary effect of a voided cloak is that other units may gain a lock-on. This cancels the double-range penalty in (G13.301) and allows units to operate under the terms of (G13.32)."],"G13.401":["Lock-on to a voided cloak","Other ships may gain a lock-on and must roll to keep it under G13.331.",["G13.4","G13.331"],"If the cloak is voided, other ships may gain a lock-on to it (except as noted). These enemy ships must roll to retain the lock-on. The die roll is in the (6B3) Lock On Stage (G13.331). See the detailed example under (G13.3323) for the precise sequence of events. Even if a lock-on is gained and retained, the five-hex penalty (G13.302) and the (G13.37) adjustment is retained. If a lock-on existed before the cloak was voided, a new die roll to retain it is not required. NOTE: In this rule, and elsewhere in the cloaking rules, the term “may be locked onto” is used. The “may be” element reflects the die roll (D6.11) that all units must make to lock onto anything. If the unit’s sensor rating is six, this is, of course, automatic."],"G13.44":["EW and cloaked ships","A cloaked ship is treated as using powerful ECM, but cannot use ECCM as its fire control is off. EW cannot be lent to it while cloaked, except during the fade-in/fade-out period.",["G13.441","G13.331"],"ELECTRONIC WARFARE: A cloaked ship is presumed to be using a powerful form of ECM and may expend additional energy for ECM."],"G13.5":["Other effects of the cloak","While cloaked or fading, a ship cannot fire, launch, guide seeking weapons, keep lock-on, use labs or use passive fire control. It can still launch shuttles and wild weasels (not in this game).",["G13.51","G13.512","G13.513"],"OTHER EFFECTS OF THE CLOAK Use of the cloaking device has certain other effects."],"G13.511":["No lock-ons","A cloaked ship cannot gain or retain a lock-on to anything.",["G13.51"],"A cloaked ship cannot gain or retain a lock-on to any object. See (D6.62) for a list of things the ship cannot do because it has no lock-on."],"G13.513":["No direct fire","A cloaked ship cannot fire direct-fire weapons.",["G13.51"],"A cloaked ship cannot fire direct-fire weapons."],"G13.514":["Seeking weapons lose tracking","Seeking weapons it controlled lose tracking when it cloaks.",["G13.512","F2.63"],"Tracking of seeking weapons will be lost (F2.63)."],"G13.6":["Advanced cloaking","Optional rules for hidden movement (the cloaked ship is removed from the board and revealed only on certain impulses), and for experience in tracking (the enemy learns over turns, starting with a +10 range penalty instead of +5).",["G13.61","G13.62"],"ADVANCED CLOAKING PROCEDURES"],"G13.63":["Entering a scenario cloaked","Optional. The cloaked ship starts fully cloaked at the specified range (47 hexes here) and speed 4, the zero speed factor. The other ships start at Weapon Status I. The Basic Set map is only 42 columns wide, so with this option the map is widened to 48 columns. A cloaked ship must still pay for its cloak on turn 1 or it fades in at once (G13.116).",["G13.12","G13.116","S4.11"]],
"G13.61":["Hidden movement","Optional. The cloaked ship's counter is removed and its position is recorded secretly. On impulses 8 and 24 each enemy ship may ask which arc and range each cloaked ship is in. Needs a moderator. Suggests a BPV advantage of about 33% for the hunter.",["G13.6","G13.611","G13.612"],"HIDDEN MOVEMENT (Optional-Experimental) This experimental rule can be used for extra excitement in hunting a cloaked ship. It is optional and requires the consent of all players. It will almost certainly require a non-playing moderator (or an extremely honest cloaked player). It is not intended to function as the sole or standard cloaking rule, but is only to be used for an interesting alternative. For purposes of play balance, the non-cloaking player should have a BPV advantage on the order of 33%, but this figure is not exact and is influenced by many factors such as terrain, weapons being used, experience of the players, and the size of the fleets."],"G13.62":["Experience in tracking","Optional. The first attack from a unit uses a +10 range penalty, which drops by one for each earlier turn that unit scored damage on the cloaked ship, down to +3.",["G13.6"],"EXPERIENCE IN TRACKING (Optional) To account for the ability of enemy units to “learn” how to detect the cloaked ships (by adjusting their instruments and discovering just what to look for), the following procedure can be used. Add ten (not five) to the range (G13.302) on the first firing attempt, and reduce this penalty by one for each previous turn during which the firing unit (not player or side) fired at and scored damage points on the cloaked (not fading) ship with direct-fire weapons in this scenario. This penalty is adjusted specifically for each firing unit and each cloaked ship it is firing at. The penalty can never be reduced below three hexes. Penalty Impulse Device On <--------------Fade---------------> Off Eff Rng Eff Rng"],"FP4.31":["Plasma built-in ECCM","All plasma torpedoes are assumed to have three points of ECCM, added to the controlling ship's ECCM if it keeps a lock-on.",["FP4.5","D6.36"],"TORPEDO: All plasma torpedoes are assumed to have three points of ECCM; see (D6.393)."],"F2.63":["Loss of tracking","A seeking weapon can lose tracking, for example when its target cloaks and it has no lock-on.",["G13.514"],"LOST LOCK-ON: If lock-on is lost to several seeking weapons and then restored, the opposing players will not know which seeking weapon is which in so far as when and by what ship it was launched, but they will be able to track seeking weapon identities from the point at which a lock-on was re-established."],"C2.42":["Maneuver rate","For cloak lock-on rolls, the speed factor uses the maneuver rate: practical speed plus the movement cost of certain actions. In this game that means a high energy turn: its 5 points count only in the impulse in which the HET is actually made (C2.421, C2.422), and an HET made before the cloak is on does not count. Braking (needs reverse movement), tactical maneuvers and erratic maneuvering (not allowed under a cloak, G13.59) are not in the game.",["G13.331"],"MANEUVER RATE: For purposes of cloaked ships (G13.331) (and all other cloaked speed calculations.), or speed restrictions to avoid voiding a wild weasel (J3.13), the movement point cost of certain functions and actions is added to the practical speed to produce the maneuver rate. These functions and actions include: High Energy Turns (C6.21), Tactical Maneuvers (C5.12) or (C5.22), Braking Energy (C3.52), and/or Erratic Maneuvering (EM) (C10.11). The maneuver rate is based on the electronic signature (specifically “exhaust”) produced by the engines; it does not include engine power used for non-movement purposes. The maneuver rate is not actually “speed” in the sense used elsewhere. The maneuver rate effectively makes it impossible for a ship protected by a WW to use EM or to perform an HET without voiding the WW. …"],"G9.21":["Crew casualties from internal damage","Every tenth internal damage point scored on a ship kills one crew unit. The last crew unit cannot be killed this way.",["G9.22","D7.21","G9.31"],"CAUSE: Every tenth internal damage point scored on a ship kills one crew unit. See also (D7.21), (G17.25), and (J4.811). If the ship is destroyed, see (G9.11). Exception: (G9.231)."],"G9.22":["Survivors","The last crew unit and the last two boarding parties cannot be destroyed by internal damage.",["G9.21","G9.431"],"SURVIVORS: The last crew unit and the last two boarding parties (in effect, two crew units) cannot be destroyed by internal damage scored against the ship. (There would almost always be survivors.) Monsters that attack crew units directly (rather than by damaging the ship) are exempt from this rule. See (SM6.47)."],"G9.31":["Crew unit record keeping","A ship's crew units include its boarding parties (and deck crews); two boarding parties equal one crew unit. The SSD crew box track is the number of crew units, and its boarding party track is the number of boarding parties.",["G9.21","D7.21"],"CREW UNITS: The number of crew units on a given ship includes the boarding parties and deck crews. Two boarding parties or deck crews equal one crew unit. It is desirable to keep track of which crew units on a given ship are boarding parties, which are deck crews, and which are general crew."],"G9.41":["Minimum crew","The minimum crew depends on the original crew units: 1-4 needs 1, 5-8 needs 2, 9-12 needs 3, 13 or more needs 4. Boarding parties and deck crews do not count toward it.",["G9.411","G9.42","G9.43"],"REQUIREMENTS: The minimum crew for each ship depends on the size of its original crew, as follows: Original crew Minimum crew 1-4 5-8 9-12 13+ Starbase"],"G9.42":["Undermanned ships","A ship below its minimum crew can only operate power producing systems, control systems, sensors, scanners and shields. ECM and ECCM still work. It can move, but its turn mode goes up by one.",["G9.421","G9.422","G9.41"],"RESTRICTIONS ON UNDERMANNED SHIPS: If a ship is undermanned, it cannot operate any equipment except power producing systems (engines, APR, batteries), control systems, sensors, scanners, and shields. The ship can use ECM and ECCM normally unless it is Uncontrolled (G2.24)."],"G9.421":["Undermanned movement","The turn mode of an undermanned ship is increased by one. One crew unit runs movement and one must man a control system; an HET would need a second crew unit for movement. (This game applies the turn mode increase and uses two crew units for movement and control.)",["G9.42","G9.422"],"It can move, but maneuverability is affected; the Turn Mode is increased by one. One crew unit can be assigned to “movement” and conduct all normal movement, turning, and sideslips, but a second unit would be required to perform an HET. There is no effect on nimble status or EM. In order for the ship to move [including tactical maneuvers (C3.0) or a “zero energy turn” (C5.13) while stationary], one crew unit must be assigned to a control system (Bridge, Aux, Emer, Flag, but not Security). If all eligible control systems have been destroyed, this crew unit may be assigned to one in any case [this is the “temporary control room” as defined in (D7.501)]. The ship in this case will also be operating under the “uncontrolled ship” (G2.2) restrictions e.g., even if a crew unit were assigned to operate a tractor beam, the tractor beam would not be useable under (G2.24)."],"G9.422":["One system per crew unit","Each crew unit can operate one undestroyed systems box, such as a weapon. In this game that applies to undermanned ships: weapons are manned in SSD order (phasers, torpedoes, disruptors, plasma) by the crew units left after movement and control, and the rest cannot fire.",["G9.42","G9.4221"],"Each crew unit on board can operate one undestroyed systems box on the SSD (a weapon, tractor beam, lab, etc.). The cloaking device requires a crew unit. UIM, aegis, improved drone control, and DERFACS do not require an extra crew unit. An SFG requires one crew unit, not two (G16.52). This one system per crew unit is in addition to the systems listed in (G9.42)."],"G9.431":["Disbanding boarding parties","If the ship is reduced to or below its minimum crew while the last two boarding parties survive, they can be converted to one general crew unit to satisfy the minimum. The game does this automatically.",["G9.22","G9.43"],"Since the last two boarding parties cannot be destroyed by damage scored on the ship (G9.22), it would be possible for the ship to have boarding parties remaining when the ship is reduced to or below a minimum crew. In this case, the owning player may disband the last two boarding parties (convert them to one general crew unit) to satisfy the minimum crew requirement. Once converted, they cannot be converted back into boarding parties, and their “last two boarding parties” exemption (G9.22) no longer applies. Deck crews"],"G9.452":["Reduced crew and repairs","A ship with less than half of its original general crew repairs as if its damage control rating were 2. The game applies that cap to the damage control rating.",["G9.451"],"Ships with less than 1/2 of their original crew (not counting boarding parties, passengers, or deck crews in the original or remaining crew) conduct (D9.4) repairs with an assumed maximum damage control rating of “2” regardless of the actual damage control rating, and no repairs to the damage control track can be made. STAR FLEET BATTLES"],"D7.21":["Boarding party casualties","Every tenth internal damage point also destroys one boarding party, but the first four such losses are ignored (so boarding parties die from the 50th internal point on). The last two cannot be destroyed this way.",["G9.21","G9.22"],"SHIP COMBAT: Every tenth internal damage point destroys one boarding party (it takes two BPs to make a crew unit), in addition to one crew unit (G9.21). The first four such boarding party casualties are ignored; the last two boarding parties on the ship cannot be killed by this method but could be broken up by the owning player (declared to be simple crew units, It takes two BPs to make a crew unit) to form part of a skeleton crew (G9.41). However, see (D7.51). The BP to be lost is selected by the owning player and can be any of his own BPs (including guards) except one captured by the enemy. Casualties under (D7.832) are scored first, before casualties resulting from general internal damage. If two or more players (all on different sides) have boarding parties on a ship that is damaged, all take casualties by this procedure."],"C6.5421":["Breakdown crew casualties","A breakdown kills one third of the crew units (rounded down), including at least a quarter of the boarding parties. The casualties cannot include the last two crew units (the last crew unit and last two boarding parties, G9.22).",["C6.542","G9.22","C6.5423"],"One third of the crew units (including at least 1/4 of the boarding parties and 1/4 of the deck crews) are killed. (This is based on the number of such units on board at the time of breakdown. Round fractions down when calculating the losses.) The full crew unit casualties must be scored, but cannot include the last two crew units (G9.22). Note that in the case of a ship with few crew units and many boarding parties (e.g., a troop ship) the ship may lose no actual crewmen but dozens of boarding parties will die like dogs. Additional crew losses may result from the resolution of internal damage. Also, 1/4 of any C — MOVEMENT enemy boarding parties on the ship are lost; they do not count toward the requirement for friendly crew or BP losses. Assigned guards are not “knocked out” but will be killed if the box they are guarding is destroyed (D7.832)."],"G9.411":["Undermanned","If the crew of a ship is reduced below this level by enemy action or by evacuation, or if the skeleton crew beamed aboard a captured ship is less than the specified size, it is considered to be “undermanned.” See (K1.31) for casualties to PF crews.",[],"If the crew of a ship is reduced below this level by enemy action or by evacuation, or if the skeleton crew beamed aboard a captured ship is less than the specified size, it is considered to be “undermanned.” See (K1.31) for casualties to PF crews."],"G9.43":["Special crew units","SPECIAL CREW UNITS: Boarding parties and deck crews cannot be counted toward the minimum crew requirements.",[],"SPECIAL CREW UNITS: Boarding parties and deck crews cannot be counted toward the minimum crew requirements."],"G9.4221":["Assigning crew units","Crew units are assigned to a system box, system repair [exception, shield repairs under (D9.2) only require that a control station be manned and power supplied] or maneuver capability at the start of the turn during the Initial Activity Phase as part of the As …",[],"Crew units are assigned to a system box, system repair [exception, shield repairs under (D9.2) only require that a control station be manned and power supplied] or maneuver capability at the start of the turn during the Initial Activity Phase as part of the Assign Boarding Parties as Guard (D7.83) segment. Note that militia (D15.83) is created in this same segment, and that boarding parties and deck crews are converted to crew units (G9.431) in this same segment. Note specifically that a given crew unit can only operate one system in a given turn. Crew units may be reassigned on the following turn, and on each subsequent turn in the same segment."],"G9.451":["Reduced crews","Ships with reduced crews may have various penalties specified elsewhere in the rules of this and other SFB products.",[],"Ships with reduced crews may have various penalties specified elsewhere in the rules of this and other SFB products."],"C4.1":["Sideslip mode","A sideslip is done during the Impulse Procedure. Every unit, at every speed, has a slip mode of 1: after it has moved one hex in a straight line since its last sideslip, it may sideslip. After a sideslip the count starts again. Turn mode and slip mode are tracked independently."],"C4.2":["Sideslip procedure","When a unit sideslips it moves into one of the two hexes ahead and to the side (forward-left or forward-right) but keeps its original facing."],"C4.31":["Hex entered","For satisfying the slip mode, the hex entered during the sideslip itself does not count."],"C4.32":["Turn mode","For satisfying the ordinary turn mode, the movement before, during and after a sideslip all counts as movement in the same direction (as straight movement)."],"C4.33":["Turning resets slip mode","A regular 60° turn resets the sideslip mode to zero; it must resume from that point. A unit may not turn and sideslip on the same impulse. The movement made as part of the turn counts as the straight movement for the slip mode."],"C4.36":["Reset","An HET, reversing direction, or stopping resets the sideslip mode (and turn mode) to zero."],"C5.13":["Zero-energy turns","A ship that had no power allocated to any movement function for the whole turn, and did not move under its own power, can make one tactical maneuver per turn at no cost, but only on impulse 32."],"C5.21":["Warp tactical maneuvers","The ship stays in its hex for the whole turn but can turn 60°, several times under certain conditions. A ship can make up to four, each costing the energy it would have taken to move one hex (C5.22)."],"C5.231":["Earning tactical maneuvers","Tactical maneuvers are resolved on the Speed 4 column of the impulse chart. A ship scheduled for four earns them on impulses 2, 8, 16 and 24."],"C5.232":["Only one unused","A ship can hold only one earned and unused tactical maneuver at a time. If it earns another before using the first, the new one is lost."],"C7.11":["Disengagement by acceleration","The ship moves for the entire turn at the maximum possible speed its warp power allows (limited to 31). At the end of that turn, if it still has total warp power equal to 50% of its original warp power (rounded up) or fifteen points, whichever is lower, the owner announces that it is disengaging. The ship is removed from the board. Ships that never had warp engines cannot disengage this way."],"C7.121":["Timing of the conditions","The conditions of C7.11 are based on the engine power available at the start of the turn. A ship that could have gone faster than it did (for example because it was held back by the acceleration limit) cannot disengage that turn; it must fly another turn at its new maximum speed first."],"C7.53":["Seeking weapons","Seeking weapons targeted on a disengaging unit lose their tracking and cannot pursue it once it has disengaged."],"C8.10":["Emergency deceleration: procedure","During any impulse a player may announce emergency deceleration. At the end of the movement segment of the second following impulse, the ship stops and is subject to the post-deceleration restrictions (C8.4)."],"C8.101":["When the ship stops","At the end of the Movement Segment of the second subsequent impulse (including impulses in which the ship does not move) the ship stops."],"C8.102":["Unused movement energy","All unused movement energy is calculated and halved (drop fractions). That amount is added to shield reinforcement at the end of the movement segment; the rest is lost. Energy allocated for HETs is not counted."],"C8.11":["Shields","The energy can go to any one shield you choose, or be divided in any way between the three shields of one group (front group: #6, #1, #2; rear group: #3, #4, #5), but not between groups. Put into general reinforcement it is worth half as much, because general reinforcement costs two energy per point."],"C8.111":["Not reserve power","The energy released by emergency deceleration is not reserve power and can only be used for the shields as described in C8.11."],"C8.112":["Calculated in power","The calculation is in energy points, not movement points or shield points. A ship with a movement cost of 1/2 gains only half as much shield energy as one with a cost of 1 (at the same speed)."],"C8.31":["Movement during deceleration","During the two impulses of deceleration the ship still counts as moving at the original speed. It must follow its plot and may still turn or sideslip if it satisfies its modes, accelerate with reserve power, perform HETs, and so on."],"C8.41":["Post-deceleration movement","For the sixteen impulses after the ship stops it cannot move out of its hex under its own power and counts as speed zero."],"C8.412":["Into the next turn","If the post-deceleration period runs into the next turn, the ship stays at zero speed for the first impulses of that turn, paying for a full turn of movement even though it does not get part of it. The unused movement energy of that period is treated as in C8.102."],"S2.2":["Victory points","Points are scored for damage inflicted, ships crippled or destroyed, and forced disengagements. A ship forced to disengage gives the enemy 25% of its BPV."]};
